WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.250
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.230
where we discuss science

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.913
and science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.230
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.320
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.240
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.030
Today, my guest is Dr. Emily Balcetis.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.220
Dr. Balcetis is a professor of psychology

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.900
at New York University,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.050
her laboratory studies
motivation, goal setting

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.230
and tools for successful goal completion.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:31.110
I learned about Dr.
Balcetis's work some years ago

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.030
because I'm a vision scientist,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.040
that is, I study the visual system,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.080
and I heard about this
incredible psychologist

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.580
at New York University

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.890
who was studying how vision,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.810
that is, how we visualize problems

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.190
can predict whether or not

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.470
we will successfully overcome challenges

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:51.090
and how we strategize in
order to set and meet goals.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.240
And in 2020, I learned
of Dr. Balcetis's book,

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.010
which was written for the general public,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.080
entitled "Clearer, Closer, Better:

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.330
How Successful People See the World".

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.610
And I read both the hard copy of the book

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.680
and listened to the audiobook,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.900
and I absolutely loved the material.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.020
As you'll learn directly
from Dr. Balcetis today,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.270
how people visualize a problem,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.270
that is, whether or not they
think of a goal or a problem

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.970
as residing at the top
of a very steep hill,

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.540
or on the top of a shallower hill,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.570
or whether or not they
visualize a goal or a problem

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.980
as far off in the distance

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.500
or closer to them in the distance,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.660
visually, in their mind,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.450
strongly dictates whether
or not they will arrive

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.400
at the challenge of meeting a goal

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.610
or overcoming a problem with
more energy or less energy.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.200
Indeed, it dictates whether or not

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.930
they can push to immediate milestones,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.910
or whether or not they will
think they have to overcome

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.800
the entire task all at once.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.910
Basically, Dr. Balcetis's
work has discovered

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:55.320
that how we visualize a
problem or a goal in our mind

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.200
has everything to do with
how we lean into that goal,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.440
whether or not we think of it
as overwhelming or tractable,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.750
whether or not we think that
we can overcome that goal

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:04.583
and then it will lead

00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:07.080
to yet more possible rewards and goals,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.460
or whether or not we feel

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.380
that we're going to
arrive at the finish line

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.570
and then just be overwhelmed with fatigue.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:16.020
In other words, how you
visualize things in your mind,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.220
and when I say, "Visualize,"

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.320
I mean, literally, how you visualize them

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.470
as a visual problem or a visual goal,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:23.850
has everything to do

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.490
with whether or not you will
be able to meet those goals

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.070
and whether or not they will
lead to still greater goals

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:30.990
that you will be able to achieve.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.390
Today's episode is an especially
important one, I believe,

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.250
because you're going to learn about

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.110
quality peer-reviewed science

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.150
from the expert in this field

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.310
of goal setting, motivation and pursuit,

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.570
and you're also going to learn

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.430
an immense number of practical tools

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.980
that you can apply toward
your educational goals,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.050
your career goals, relationship goals,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.790
goals of any sort.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.200
By the end of today's episode,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.000
you will be better equipped

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.490
to set and achieve your goals.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.850
Dr. Balcetis also shares
with us her own experiences

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:03.660
of how to set, visualize
and achieve goals,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.640
and she does that within the context

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.530
of her role as a parent,

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:10.080
as somebody navigating
relationships of various kinds,

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:11.550
and a demanding career.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.160
So again, I think that you'll
find the information today

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.850
to be both extremely academically grounded

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.260
in terms of research,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.320
extremely practical and realistic

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.780
in terms of how you might
apply it in your own life.

