00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.650
And then, as time goes on,

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.060
I may have some additional questions

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.530
as it relates to
different kinds of vision,

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.960
but what's the deal with
vision and motivation?

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.280
How do those two things link up?

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.927
- Yeah, totally, I mean, when
psychologists ask people,

00:08:56.927 --> 00:08:58.050
like, "What are you doing

00:08:58.050 --> 00:08:59.520
to help make progress on your goals?"

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.353
they say all kinds of things,

00:09:00.353 --> 00:09:02.370
but a couple things always pop to the top,

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:05.040
which is, "Try to shock
myself in encouraging ways,

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.810
and self-pep talks,"

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.440
or, "I remind myself of how
important it is to do this job,"

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:13.650
or, "I'll put up Post-it notes around

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:16.530
to constantly be nagging me
about what I need to do."

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.300
So those are common
tactics that people use,

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.640
and what we'll notice is that
those are really effortful,

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.140
having to constantly remind yourself,

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.580
having to constantly talk to yourself,

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:25.050
having to create those Post-it notes,

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:25.920
remember to look at them,

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.250
all of that takes a lot of
time and effort and commitment,

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.100
and so what a surprise that
people burn out, right?

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.070
It's exciting to work on a
goal when you first set it,

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:36.570
you might make some initial progress,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:40.560
but then eventually we get
not even to the halfway point

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.870
before things get real, [laughing]

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:44.130
things are challenging,

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:45.360
and we fall by the wayside.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.550
And that's, I think, because those tactics

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.920
that are our go-to strategies

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.960
are themselves a goal to maintain.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.530
So it's double sided, we're
putting so much on ourselves

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:57.120
to try to advance the thing

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.730
that we originally set out to accomplish.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.770
So then, with my team, I
was trying to think of,

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.030
like, "Well, what are strategies

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.290
that don't require as much effort,

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:05.130
that we can automate,

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:07.170
that we can take advantage
of what's already happening

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:10.080
within ourselves, within
our body, within our mind,

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.080
that might overcome one
of those challenges,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.150
that'll be easier, more automated?"

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:16.230
And that's when we started to land

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:17.820
on the idea of vision, right?

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.510
We look at the world
without even thinking of it,

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.443
for those of us that are sighted,

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:24.900
and we thought, "You know what?

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.060
There are strategies that we can use

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:29.100
to look at the world in a different way

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.510
and that we can automate

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:34.440
that might help us to
overcome some obstacles,

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:35.730
to make progress on our goals,

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.100
to maybe literally see opportunities

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.260
that we hadn't been able to see before."

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.460
So we started playing around

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.397
with the idea of visual illusions to see,

00:10:43.397 --> 00:10:44.820
like, "Do people even know

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.070
that there's other ways of
seeing things around them?

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:49.650
Can we tweak that, or is
there room for intervention?

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:53.640
Can we encourage people to
take a new way of looking

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.440
to see things that they
hadn't seen before?"

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:56.790
And that's what really opened us up

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:58.350
to trying to look at that intersection

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:02.820
between vision science
and motivation science.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.190
- That's great, and I always say,

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.890
and here, I'm strongly
biased as a vision scientist,

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.540
that vision is the dominant sense

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.670
by which we navigate
the world and survive.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:16.170
I love this idea of real-world,
realtime access to vision.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:17.370
And I'm certainly familiar

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:21.360
with how goal setting or Post-its

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:22.650
and magnets on refrigerators

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.720
can have an immediate
impact, but then over time,

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.880
they become so part of
the visual landscape

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:27.713
that you overlook them,

00:11:27.713 --> 00:11:29.010
and we know, as vision scientists,

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.350
if something is stably
in your environment,

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:33.270
eventually, you're blind to it,

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:34.770
so that makes good sense.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:38.010
So you've published a number
of studies in this area,

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.833
but maybe you could
highlight some of the more,

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.460
what you would consider important findings

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.330
in the area of how people
can adjust their vision

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.720
in order to meet goals more
quickly and more efficiently,

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:56.460
and perhaps also how
we all arrive at goals

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.590
with different visual perceptions,

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.450
and that, in some way,

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.060
may divide us into highly motivated

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:04.080
and less motivated people,

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:05.610
in other words, what's the link

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:07.230
between vision and motivation,

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:08.370
and how can we leverage that

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:09.540
in order to better reach our goals?

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.864
- Totally, so we started thinking about,

00:12:12.864 --> 00:12:14.580
"What are the goals that
are most important to people

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:16.410
that they struggle with the most?"

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.410
So we asked hundreds, thousands of people

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:20.820
what their new year's resolutions are,

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:22.560
we looked to all the other polls

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:24.300
that do the same kind of work,

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:25.650
and regardless of where you look,

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:27.420
or who you ask, or when you ask it,

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:28.410
people's number-one goal

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.480
is something related
to their health, right?

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.507
To lose weight, to exercise more,

00:12:33.507 --> 00:12:34.770
to get out, get more steps,

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:36.873
for mental wellbeing, physical wellbeing.

00:12:38.027 --> 00:12:42.060
And that's like the number-one
goal every January 1st.

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:44.610
So if we were able to
accomplish that goal,

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:45.568
you'd think it would drop

00:12:45.568 --> 00:12:46.710
[laughing] a little bit in the rankings,

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.050
but it doesn't because it's really hard.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:50.280
So we thought, "I wonder if there's a way

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.590
for us to make some progress on that,

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.450
on helping people to exercise better,

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:56.160
more often, stick to it longer,

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.380
and make some progress there?"

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.000
We know diets don't work. [laughing]

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.170
Why don't diets work?

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.930
For the same reason that
that self-talk doesn't work,

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:08.220
is that we go in it, full bore, hardcore,

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:10.260
and it requires a major
commitment and effort

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:11.790
to a lifestyle change.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.200
So again, we were looking for something

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.030
that might be easier than that,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.490
that could produce big payoff, right?

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.110
That's the golden ticket, [laughing]

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:22.710
is something that requires less
effort for a bigger payoff.

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:24.270
So one of the first things that I did

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.310
was go over to Brooklyn,

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:28.203
to this old armory building,

00:13:29.363 --> 00:13:31.260
it used to be a military
armory space, yeah.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.100
- I think I know that building.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:33.540
- Yeah, it's beautiful.
- It's a beautiful building,

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.280
now, that houses a lot
of businesses, right?

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.368
With plants on the walls,

00:13:36.368 --> 00:13:37.290
is that the one?
- Yeah, there's businesses,

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:38.123
there's a couple of armories

00:13:38.123 --> 00:13:41.523
all around the boroughs
here around New York City,

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.630
and the one in Brooklyn in
particular is now YMCA, right?

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:47.250
So it's a family YMCA,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.740
that's within this beautiful
old redbrick building

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:53.130
that used to be a military
establishment long, long ago.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:57.330
And what's really cool is
that, one winter afternoon,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.187
somebody had invited me,
a physical therapist said,

00:14:00.187 --> 00:14:01.020
"Hey, you should come out

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.100
and check out what's happening here,

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.480
with your interest in exercise

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.790
and trying to find new
ways of helping people,

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.400
new tactics that they can
add to their tool belt,

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:09.870
I think you're going to
find some interesting people

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.030
that are working out there."

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.810
So I showed up, I look around,

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.990
there's families, there's
new moms, there's kids,

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.580
moms trying to get kids to burn off

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:20.310
some winter energy that they have,

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:22.830
there's people that look like
they're just there for their,

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.540
every couple of days, going out for a run.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:25.440
There's some people that look like

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:26.820
they're training with a team,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.300
and that's who this physical
therapist introduced me to,

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.040
was the coach of this team.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.300
There's a bunch of people
that were sitting down

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:34.133
on the ground,

00:14:34.133 --> 00:14:35.670
and I would be hard pressed

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:37.110
to know who is the high school student

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:37.943
that's in this group,

00:14:37.943 --> 00:14:39.720
and then who, as it turns out,

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.570
are some of the fastest
runners in the world.

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.970
One of the people that
was in the last Olympics

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:48.390
before I showed up, won the
gold medal for the 400 meter.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.400
And from the looks of them,

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:51.420
I mean, of course,

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.850
their bodies are in
better shape than mine,

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:56.190
but there's nothing so,

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:57.300
of course, they're not
wearing their medals,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:58.133
there's nothing pretentious

00:14:58.133 --> 00:15:00.030
about how they're walking
around or anything like that,

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:00.863
that would lead me to know,

00:15:00.863 --> 00:15:02.040
like, "This person's amazing,

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.680
and they probably have some
insight that I don't have."

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.150
So once I got introduced to them

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:07.860
and knew who are these people

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.490
that were part of this
pretty elite training team

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:13.953
that happened to work
out at this family gym,

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:16.867
I had the chance to talk with them about,

00:15:16.867 --> 00:15:18.870
"What strategies do you use?"

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:20.940
Now, I am not an elite runner,

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.980
and having recently had a baby,

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:24.720
I'm not really a runner right now at all,

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.390
but I thought, "When
these people are running,

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:29.190
I bet they are hyper aware

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.320
of everything that's going
on in their surroundings.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.750
Where are they relative
to the competition,

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:35.730
what's happening in
their peripheral vision,

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:36.840
what's going on on the side,

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:39.060
who's behind them, who's in front of them?

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.070
They probably have this master sense,

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:43.680
this master visual plan
at any point in time,

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.170
and that's what probably
makes them elite."

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.840
So when I started asking
them, "Is that the case,

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.540
do you really pay attention to
what's in your surroundings,

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:52.800
what's behind you, what's on the side?"

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.030
They said, "No,"

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:57.090
all of them said, "No, and
sometimes when I do do that,

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.100
it's a mistake, it doesn't work for me."

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:00.090
So that was surprising

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:03.210
and totally went against my
intuition about what they do

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.850
that likely contributes to their success.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.760
What they said instead was
that they are hyper focused,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:11.040
they assume this narrowed
focus of attention,

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:14.880
almost like a spotlight
is shining on a target.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:16.140
Now, when they're
running a short distance,

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:18.000
that target might literally
be the finish line,

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.560
the line that they're trying to cross.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:21.780
If it's a longer distance,
they set subgoals,

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.480
like the person, the shorts
on the person up ahead

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:25.410
that they're trying to beat,

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.080
or they choose some
sort of stable landmark,

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.960
like a sign that they would pass by.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.120
And a spotlight is shining just on that,

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.280
or they have blinders on
the sides of their face,

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:37.263
that's all they're paying attention to,

00:16:37.263 --> 00:16:40.173
this really narrowed scope of attention.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.220
And that was a strategy that
all of these elite athletes

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:45.053
said that they used,

00:16:45.053 --> 00:16:48.330
and those that were
better rather than slower

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:50.280
were ones that used it more.

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.450
And I thought, "Oh, that's something

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:52.740
we can play with, right?

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.230
They are elite [laughing]
and they are accomplished,

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:56.550
but that visual strategy

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:57.720
isn't necessarily something

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.910
that you have to be in the
perfect physical condition

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:01.200
to be able to adopt,

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.390
and so I wonder, can
that help the rest of us

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.670
who aren't competing for an Olympic gold

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.170
and who have no chance
of ever getting one,

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.870
but who want to exercise better,

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:11.280
have a better time doing it,

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:12.870
and maintain a commitment

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:14.400
to that exercise goal
that they might have,

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:15.840
that they might otherwise,

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:18.990
by February or March, be giving up on,

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:21.660
if they had said it at
the beginning of January?"

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.460
So that's really where the work started,

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.280
was what you might call focus groups

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.063
or case studies [laughing]
of these incredible athletes.

00:17:30.416 --> 00:17:31.380
And then we did other studies,

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:33.720
looking at people who
aren't Olympic athletes,

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:38.220
but who are competitive and
New York Road Runners runners,

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:40.080
and how are they running in races?

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:40.913
And what we found is that those people

00:17:40.913 --> 00:17:44.340
who have better pace,
faster pace, better time,

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.930
they use that narrowed strategy

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:48.570
more often than this more expansive

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:51.390
or open scope of attention.

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:52.650
And there seemed to be a correlation

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.150
between that better performance

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.717
among a wider swath of
hundreds of runners,

00:17:57.717 --> 00:17:59.310
who are doing it competitively,

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:01.110
but still, could be like the person

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:03.060
that you're sitting next to in the office,

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:04.830
or yourself, right?

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:07.050
And the more often that they did it

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:10.170
and the more consistently
they had adopted that,

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:12.270
that technique of the
narrowed focus of attention,

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:14.870
it seemed that they were
doing better in their runs.

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:17.767
So then we started thinking like,

00:18:17.767 --> 00:18:21.120
"Okay, what about people who
aren't competitive runners?

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.210
What about like my mom,
[laughing] can she do that,

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.640
or me when I'm trying to
get back on the bandwagon

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:28.140
and exercise more,

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.060
is this a tactic we can teach people?"

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:32.640
The answer is yes, you can tell people

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:34.470
about what these Olympic
athletes are doing,

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:35.303
you can tell them about

00:18:35.303 --> 00:18:38.550
what the New York Road
Runners runners are doing,

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:39.750
and just using the same language

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:40.747
that I just used with you, right?

00:18:40.747 --> 00:18:43.500
"Imagine that there's a spotlight
shining just on a target,

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:45.210
choose something up ahead,

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:49.140
the stop sign two blocks
up that you can just see,

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:51.780
and imagine that you have blinders on

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.130
so that you're not really paying attention

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.330
to the people that are passing by,

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:56.280
or the buildings, or the garbage cans,

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:58.710
or the trucks that are on the road,

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.890
tune those out and
focus in on that target,

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:04.020
until you hit it, and then
choose another one, right?

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.177
Sort of recalibrate,
choose the next goal."

00:19:07.355 --> 00:19:09.450
And so we would test,
like, "Can people do that?"

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:10.710
I mean, if you're listening right now,

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.900
you probably are imagining
that experience too,

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:13.770
and the answer is yes,

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:15.810
like, "I can imagine that, I
know what those words mean,

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:17.100
and I can do that."

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.033
And our work found that too,

00:19:18.033 --> 00:19:20.287
that people can do that,
we have them say out loud,

00:19:20.287 --> 00:19:22.560
"What is it that's
captured your attention?"

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.360
And of course, "Sometimes
something in the periphery,

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:26.130
like movement, captures our gaze

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:28.530
and we are pulled there for an instant,

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:30.510
but then we can refocus up again

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:32.790
and adopt that narrowed attention."

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:34.410
Now, one of the first studies that we did

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:38.250
was teach that strategy
and juxtapose or compare it

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:39.577
against a group that we said,

00:19:39.577 --> 00:19:41.430
"Just look around naturally,

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:43.380
you might see that finish line up ahead

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:44.700
and there's things on the periphery,

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.020
whatever your eyes want to do,

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.250
whatever you think is going to work best,

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:50.021
feel free to do that, and tell
us what you're looking at."

00:19:50.021 --> 00:19:51.060
Then we gave them a finish line,

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.310
we created sort of an exercise

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:56.040
that's moderately
challenging, but possible.

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:57.720
We put ankle weights on

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.910
that accounted for about
15% of their body weight,

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:01.200
told them to lift their knees up,

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:03.540
sort of high stepping to a finish line.

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:06.990
So this would be
challenging for them to do,

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:08.730
but we said, "It's an indicator

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:10.377
of overall health and fitness."

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:13.320
Some of these people had
narrowed their focus of attention

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:17.700
and some were just looking
more expansively or naturally.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:19.530
And what we found is that
those people that we trained,

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:21.240
just everyday normal people

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:24.270
doing this moderately
challenging exercise,

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:26.580
they were able to move 27% faster.

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.230
They could do the exercise more quickly,

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:31.650
and they said it hurt 17% less.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.200
The exercise was exactly the
same for all of the people,

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:36.750
we set the weight, [laughing]
we set the distance,

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:38.370
it was in our lab space,

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.560
so it was a constrained environment,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.350
everybody was in the same
sort of circumstance,

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:45.510
but yet their experience
was really different,

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.340
we helped them to move faster,

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:49.110
burn calories at a higher rate, right?

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:50.580
Exercise more efficiently,

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:51.690
the amount of time they put in

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:55.710
is going to produce a
better physical outcome,

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:57.300
and also, it didn't hurt them, right?

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.340
They're saying, "It doesn't hurt as much."

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.470
So we were really excited
about that, right?

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:02.790
Because it meant that this strategy,

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:05.880
we could use it on people
who were not elite athletes.

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:07.410
It could be easily adopted,

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:09.240
a quick training session, right,

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:12.000
can teach people to look at
the world in a different way.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.280
Again, this narrowed
attention was different

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:17.850
than whatever they do
naturally, the comparison group,

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:19.260
but it had a big outcome,

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:20.760
it had a big difference on the way

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:23.100
that they were engaged in the exercise.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:24.840
It was some of the first work that we did,

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:26.820
and then, since then,
we've done, I don't know,

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.177
dozens more studies to look at,

00:21:28.177 --> 00:21:29.340
"Well, what happens with that

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.340
and what else can we do with
playing around with this?"

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:34.710
- Yeah, those are impressive differences

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.320
as a consequence of
narrowing visual attention.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:39.090
Couple of questions
about the actual practice

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.380
of narrowing attention,

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:44.250
is there any indication
of whether or not subjects

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:47.430
are constantly updating
their visual attention?

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:52.020
So for instance, if let's
say the goal line is in view,

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:53.400
literally, from the beginning,

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.230
I could imagine just
holding visual attention

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:56.790
on the goal line,

00:21:56.790 --> 00:22:00.390
but if it's a oval track,

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:03.930
or it's a trajectory along
a trail, or through a city,

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:06.280
how often do you think they are updating

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:11.160
their visual aperture and
setting a visual goal?

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.320
And I could imagine

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.140
that there's some
energetic expense to that,

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:19.800
so meaning, you wouldn't want to do

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:21.060
every crack on the sidewalk,

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:22.350
unless those cracks on the sidewalk

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:23.640
were very far apart.
- [laughing] Right.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:24.690
- Because I think, at some point,

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:27.660
that itself would be exhausting.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:30.420
So is there an optimal strategy

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.000
or a semi-optimal strategy?

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.820
- Yeah, so those Olympic athletes

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:37.110
that we started by interviewing,

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:38.190
they tended to be sprinters,

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:40.650
they were more often sprinters,
short-distance sprinters.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:42.690
So when they said like, "Yes, I narrow in

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:45.420
more than I assume an expansive focus,"

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.760
that's because they're
not going that far, right?

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:49.800
They have to do it as
fast as humanly possible,

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:51.420
but they're not going that far.

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:53.220
And so we started asking
that question too,

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.110
about, like, "Well,
wouldn't that be tiring?"

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:56.400
And the answer is yes,

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:57.233
so when we start to look at,

00:22:57.233 --> 00:22:58.530
well, people who aren't sprinters,

00:22:58.530 --> 00:22:59.363
who are accomplished,

00:22:59.363 --> 00:23:01.500
but who are more long-distance runners,

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:03.120
that's what we find that they do,

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:05.850
is that they're using

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:08.460
that narrowed attention
strategy strategically,

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:10.770
and it increases in use,

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:13.320
they use it more often
as the race progresses,

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:17.018
and they really start to do
this major switch [laughing]

00:23:17.018 --> 00:23:20.760
at about the halfway point of
say like a 10-kilometer run.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.230
So people who are seasoned runners,

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:23.940
they really start making a switch

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:27.150
with what they're looking
at about halfway through,

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:29.100
and that's where they more
often, more frequently

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:30.450
and are more intentionally adopting

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:32.280
a narrowed focus of attention

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.160
when they're in the last
couple miles of a run,

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:38.100
when maybe their resources
are starting to get more thin,

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:40.590
maybe their motivation
is starting to fade.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.150
That tipping point in the middle is,

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:44.850
with any kind of goal, where
people struggle the most,

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:47.250
and that's when they're doubling down

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.800
on a strategy that they
know to be effective.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:52.680
So at first, longer-distance runners

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:54.840
are not using that narrowed strategy,

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:57.093
they're looking more expansively,

00:23:58.020 --> 00:23:59.580
because I think that that,

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.710
well, first of all, distraction
[laughing] is a thing,

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:02.543
it's useful.

00:24:02.543 --> 00:24:04.440
Not necessarily that they're
distracting themselves,

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:07.710
because people are still
trying to hold pace and jostle

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:11.130
among probably a more
concentrated group of runners,

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:12.630
but it is a strategy that they use,

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:16.650
and then sort of wean off
of as the race goes through.

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:18.960
And it's particularly effective

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:20.910
when we're looking for
that last push, right?

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:22.530
The last push to get over the finish line,

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.200
when you might be literally
neck and neck with somebody

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:28.140
that you're trying to just beat out,

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:29.910
or when you're most tired,

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:33.393
but you know that last push,
you don't want to drop off,

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:36.330
and when you want to push through hard

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:37.650
through that finish
line, that's when people

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:41.070
are using it at its
peak level of intensity.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.093
- I see, yeah.

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:46.173
To me, this makes total
sense why it would work,

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:50.550
without going down the rabbit
hole of visual neuroscience,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:51.990
something for another time,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:54.060
that when we do these
vergence eye movements,

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:55.950
when we bring our eyes to a visual target,

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:57.930
it's clear that some of
the brainstem circuitry

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:02.930
for alertness gets engaged
to a greater degree.

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.010
The other thing is that we know

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:06.690
that when we focus on an object,

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:09.690
that the optics of the eye change

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:11.640
and narrow the visual field.

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:14.430
So that brings about, this
is a very detailed question,

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:16.500
but I'm sure the audience is wondering,

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:18.480
if let's say I'm focused on a goal line

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:21.030
or an intermediate goal,

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:22.980
are they focusing on a specific point

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:25.560
or is it kind of the entire
horizon of that goal?

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.610
Because the finish line is indeed a line.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:30.810
And of course, it's impossible to know

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.480
what someone is actually
doing in their mind's eye,

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:34.740
but how do people report this?

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:37.200
Do they see it literally as a spotlight?

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:39.420
And if so, how broad is that spot?

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.930
- Yeah, so what is the length [laughing]

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.930
of their aperture rather
than maybe the diameter

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:48.873
or the sphere size of it?

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.540
In our interviews with people,

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:53.613
our sort of focus group studies,

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:57.600
it seems like it's more
like a circular point.

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:00.030
And that's in fact what
we're teaching people,

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:01.350
what we're training them to do,

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:03.150
so rather than going broadly,

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:06.180
looking across a line from left to right,

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:09.840
we are encouraging them to
imagine a circle of light

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:11.370
that's shining on some target.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:13.200
Now, of course, a finish line is a line,

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:14.370
but if they're staying in their lane,

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:15.420
if they're on a track, right?

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:18.510
You can imagine that
there is a circle shining

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:20.970
just on where in their lane
they'll cross that finish line,

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.170
or if it's a stop sign,

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:24.900
you could imagine a circle
of light illuminating that.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:26.550
So that's what we're
teaching people to use

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.410
and that's what seems to be effective

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.450
to maintain that focus

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:32.250
rather than sort of being pulled

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:34.920
to engage with peripheral vision.

00:26:34.920 --> 00:26:36.900
And there's some amazing people,

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.420
some runners in history,
like Joan Benoit Samuelson,

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:43.320
she's one of the first
female marathon competitors,

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.960
who has won multiple marathons.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:48.330
She's Canadian, I think she's won,

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:51.930
feel free to correct me, like
10 marathons in her life.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:55.110
And she talks about sort of not assuming

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:59.520
this wide but narrow, [laughing]

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.940
wide but not deep or
tall attentional focus.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:05.190
She talks about, like, "Finding the shorts

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:06.150
on somebody ahead of me

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:08.160
and focusing on those shorts,"

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:10.680
until she passes them, and
then resetting that goal.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:14.250
So in her interviews that she's
done with runners magazines,

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:17.760
she talks about it in terms
of this circle of attention.

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.433
- Mm, I think I've
experienced this a little bit,

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:24.000
because we're visiting New
York now to do this interview,

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:27.210
and runners here seem more competitive,

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:30.030
recreational runners here
seem more competitive.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:31.860
People walking on the
street seem competitive.

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:33.180
- Yeah. [laughing]
- You're walking at near pace

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:34.760
to somebody, they'll quickly speed up.

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:36.070
If you speed up, they'll speed up.

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:36.990
- Yeah.
- I think there have been

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:39.780
some studies about walking
speed in different cities,

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:43.200
and New York ranks among
the fastest walkers around.

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:45.062
I won't mention the
slowest-walking cities.

00:27:45.062 --> 00:27:46.320
[Emily laughing]
'Cause we don't want to

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:47.400
cast any judgments.

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:52.020
But fascinating, and
again, makes total sense

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:53.910
based on the way that the visual system

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:55.240
measures both space and time.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.580
- Yeah.
- Something maybe we'll get

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:57.930
into a little bit later,

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:02.640
but I'm curious whether
or not the whole thing

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:03.990
works in reverse as well.

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:08.990
Meaning, do people who are
very motivated to exercise,

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:14.910
do they think this way naturally?

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:16.920
People who are averse to exercise

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:20.730
or who find it hard to
get motivated to exercise,

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:23.760
do they view the world differently,

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.110
literally?
- Yeah,

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:27.630
yeah, I have so much that
I can say about this,

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.190
so [laughing] if you'll humor me,

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:31.620
I'll give you a couple
different stories about

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:32.790
how we can answer that.
- Please.

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:34.770
- So you don't have to do a
deep dive into vision science,

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:36.920
which, of course, you
are capable of doing,

00:28:37.950 --> 00:28:39.420
but what I can share with you

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:41.550
is some animal studies where this work

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:43.470
kind of first started.

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:45.540
This is in the 1940s, 1950s,

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:47.880
rat labs, mice labs.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:49.050
And they were looking,

00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:53.310
those were the first
models of human behavior

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:55.560
that people were trying
to understand motivation,

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:57.630
motivation science within.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:02.100
So they would deprive
these poor rats and mice

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:03.480
of food or water,

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:06.270
so that they were motivated
to get it. [laughing]

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:08.370
They were hungry and they were thirsty

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:10.200
and they had practice running a maze

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:12.150
so they knew where they could
find that food or water,

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:14.040
whatever that they were looking for.

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:15.510
And what these researchers were studying

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:19.440
was the pace of movement through the maze.

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:23.850
So as the rats were
going through the maze,

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:27.270
they found that even though
these rats were hungry

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:32.270
and they're having to expend
limited caloric energy

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:34.860
to make it to the finish
line, they actually ran faster

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:37.260
the closer they got to that finish line.

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:41.310
So once that finish line
became nearer to them,

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:43.770
they actually used their resources,

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:45.450
probably suboptimally,

00:29:45.450 --> 00:29:46.800
to make sure that they
crossed the finish line

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:48.330
and got their reward.

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:50.550
So that was some of the first early work

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:54.270
that was showing that proximity to a goal

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:57.570
increases the investment
in resources that people,

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:00.840
that animals use to meet that goal,

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.760
even when they don't have that much spare.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:05.250
And with the mice, the same kind of thing,

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:07.170
they actually had these
little harnesses on them,

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:10.050
they were looking at how
hard do the mice pull

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:11.940
to try to make it to the food or the water

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:13.350
that they were trying to get.

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:16.770
And same deal, the closer they
got to getting their reward,

00:30:16.770 --> 00:30:18.540
the harder they were pulling,

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:21.840
even though they didn't have
that much energy to spare

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.520
and they had already used some up

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:25.680
getting to that finish line.

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:30.680
So that early animal research
from the 1940s, 1950s,

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:34.170
then spurred a whole
wave of work in humans.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:35.550
Do humans do the same thing?

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:38.070
Even when they're tired, but they can see

00:30:38.070 --> 00:30:40.440
or they can feel that their goal is close,

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:41.880
do they double down and work even harder

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:43.260
to cross that finish line,

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:44.730
either a literal finish line,

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:45.840
if we're talking about exercise,

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:47.100
or a metaphorical finish line

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:48.630
if we're talking about
any other kind of goal

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:49.740
that people might have?

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:51.000
And the answer is yes,

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.490
they called that the
goal gradient hypothesis,

00:30:53.490 --> 00:30:55.410
the closer you get to the goal,

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:57.780
generally, the harder
people and animals work

00:30:57.780 --> 00:30:59.313
to finish that goal.

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:01.927
That's what led us then to think,

00:31:01.927 --> 00:31:04.350
"Okay, those rats, those mice,

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:07.080
those people are seeing
a finish line, right?

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.270
And it's when they're maybe
seeing that finish line,

00:31:09.270 --> 00:31:10.890
seeing that reward, seeing the goal

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:12.330
they're hoping to accomplish,

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:15.180
that is what's leading them to try harder,

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:18.690
to invest more so that
they can finish it off.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:22.050
What if we induce that
illusion of proximity?

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:23.670
What if we can induce a visual illusion,

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:26.850
a visual experience that approximates

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:31.170
what the real rats and mice
were actually experiencing

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:32.910
as they got closer?"

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:35.220
So that is what is happening,

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:36.420
that's what's happening visually

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.700
when we create that
narrowed focus of attention,

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:40.470
when we tell people,
"Imagine there's a spotlight

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:41.970
on the shorts of the person up ahead,

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:43.800
or the stop sign that you're seeing,"

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:46.740
it induces an illusion of proximity

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:50.520
that then is responsible
for people trying harder,

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:53.610
walking faster, or feeling that
it defied their expectations

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:55.860
and that it wasn't as bad
as they thought it would be.

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:57.450
So we do things like measure,

00:31:57.450 --> 00:32:00.000
like measure their visual experience,

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.280
like, "How far away is that finish line?"

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:04.027
Of course, we can ask
them to report in feet,

00:32:04.027 --> 00:32:05.760
"How many feet is it?"

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:06.960
Ah, but that's challenging, right?

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:08.970
Nobody really knows what three feet

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:10.410
versus four feet really looks like,

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:12.600
but they do, so we can ask
them how many feet it is.

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:14.490
We also use these other measures of,

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:15.930
like visual matching measures

00:32:15.930 --> 00:32:18.330
to know that distance to the finish line

00:32:18.330 --> 00:32:21.330
looks about as far away
as this other target,

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:23.550
their matching up their
visual experiences.

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:26.190
So what we know is that inducing

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:27.990
that narrowed focus of attention

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:30.030
is creating an illusion of proximity,

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:32.220
that goal looks closer to them.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:34.320
And then there's all kinds of downstream,

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:36.570
motivational and psychological
effects that happen

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:38.883
from feeling like you're closer,

00:32:40.234 --> 00:32:43.140
by visually misperceiving that space,

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.600
it can have a really positive consequence.

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:48.870
So your first question
was, "Which way does it go?

00:32:48.870 --> 00:32:49.710
Does it go both ways,

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:50.910
that people who are better runners

00:32:50.910 --> 00:32:52.560
happen to do this thing?"

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:54.840
Yes, some of our research shows that,

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:57.480
that if they, for whatever reason,

00:32:57.480 --> 00:32:58.950
happened upon this strategy

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:01.110
and continued to practice it,

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:04.080
they tended to be the better runners.

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:07.330
But we also know from our
experiments in the lab,

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:10.410
where we take people who don't
know about these strategies,

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:11.850
and by a flip of the coin,

00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:13.170
we randomly assign them

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:15.960
to either learn the strategy and use it

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:18.510
or do whatever comes naturally to them,

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:20.460
we can create that illusion of proximity,

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:23.520
that has a direct and causal impact

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:26.820
on improving their performance
when they're exercising.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.310
So yes, it goes both ways,

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:31.020
but you can also teach yourself

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:33.180
that you don't have to just rely on luck,

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:34.830
luck of the draw for being a person

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:36.750
who happens to be better at exercising

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:39.420
or whose eyes happen to
do this on their own.

