00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:03.300
The most pressing question
I have in my mind is,

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:07.590
can we, I, all of us,

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:12.120
use this strategy to make the
starting line a goal point?

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:13.470
Because for a lot of people,

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:15.540
it's not about going from start to finish,

00:35:15.540 --> 00:35:17.940
it's about getting to start.

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:22.410
And I would say, here, I'm estimating,

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:26.010
but 15% of the content on social media

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:30.243
is about motivation and how
to get motivated to do things.

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:34.140
Neurochemicals like dopamine, of course,

00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:35.910
being at the heart of motivation.

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:37.680
In my mind, I'm making strong links

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:39.540
between some of these
visual aperture effects

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:40.890
and goal lines and dopamine

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:43.650
that we could also dive into.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.320
But the simple question is,

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:49.620
can I use this finish line strategy

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:52.680
to make the start line a goal

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:56.220
and get my system more
engaged or motivated?

00:35:56.220 --> 00:35:58.860
And is there any physiology,

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:00.930
or physiological changes, I should say,

00:36:00.930 --> 00:36:02.190
to reflect the idea

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:05.130
that maybe just visually
focusing on the start line

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:07.650
would actually get me more excited

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:10.920
as opposed to make me less
excited to engage in effort?

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:12.540
- Mm-hmm, there's certainly vision science

00:36:12.540 --> 00:36:16.860
that's tied up in that very
first stage of goal setting,

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:19.080
like identifying what that
goal is in the first place

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:21.300
and taking those first steps.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:24.090
A lot of people's go-to
strategies that involve vision

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:28.650
are vision boards, or dream
boards, or Post-it notes, right?

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:31.590
They're creating some sort
of visual representation

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:33.727
of what it is that they
want to accomplish,

00:36:33.727 --> 00:36:35.940
"Where is it that I want
to be in five years,

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:37.440
10 days, 10 years?"

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:40.440
Whatever that timeline is
that they're working under.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.300
The idea of vision boards or dream boards

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:44.850
is that you, almost like
a scrapbook, [laughing]

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:48.000
collect visual icons that
reflect where you want to be

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:49.110
to motivate yourself.

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:52.710
It's a really common
tactic that people use,

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:54.510
and it's not bad to do that, right?

00:36:54.510 --> 00:36:57.090
For some people, just even
knowing what they want in life

00:36:57.090 --> 00:36:58.380
is a major accomplishment,

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:01.380
defining the goal can be
really challenging for people,

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:02.970
and that's a strategy that works

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:05.580
and involves our visual experience, right?

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:06.967
It's not just, people aren't saying like,

00:37:06.967 --> 00:37:09.090
"Why don't you just sit around and imagine

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:11.310
what you want your life
to be like in 10 years?"

00:37:11.310 --> 00:37:13.627
The strategy that people
are suggesting is like,

00:37:13.627 --> 00:37:15.540
"No, cut out the pictures,

00:37:15.540 --> 00:37:18.240
put it on a board and stick
it by your bathroom mirror

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:19.320
so you see it every day,"

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:20.400
right?
- Or make a list.

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:21.660
- Or make a list, yeah.
- People are big

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.292
on these lists, I have a lot of friends

00:37:23.292 --> 00:37:25.500
that are like, "Have you made your list?"

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:27.540
The list of things that
you insist on having

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:31.260
in the context of fitness,
relationship, job,

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.090
et cetera, et cetera,
this seems more and more

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:34.500
common now, yeah.
- Yeah, totally, and the idea,

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:35.340
like, "Write it down," right?

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.170
They're telling you, "Write it down,"

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:40.587
or, "Create a visual manifestation of it."

00:37:41.670 --> 00:37:46.670
And so yeah, that's effective
for identifying what you want,

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.600
but it may not actually be effective

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:50.190
for helping you to meet the goal,

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:51.840
to get the job done.

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:54.330
So colleagues of mine
at New York University

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:56.490
have probed, "Well, why, why is that?

00:37:56.490 --> 00:37:59.880
Why is just thinking about
what you want in your life

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:04.440
and sort of putting yourself
vicariously into those shoes,

00:38:04.440 --> 00:38:05.940
imagining what my life will be like

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:09.180
if I can accomplish
everything on this list,

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:10.290
why doesn't that work?"

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:11.310
Well, first of all, "Does it work?"

00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:14.970
The answer is no, "And
why does it not work?"

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:16.650
Because what happens,

00:38:16.650 --> 00:38:18.810
these colleagues, Gabriele Oettingen

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:21.030
and her research team have found

00:38:21.030 --> 00:38:25.143
is that going through and dreaming about

00:38:25.143 --> 00:38:27.300
or, "Visualizing how great my life will be

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:29.337
when I get X, Y and Z done,"

00:38:31.319 --> 00:38:34.057
that is like a goal satisfied.

00:38:34.057 --> 00:38:36.030
"I have identified what it is that I want,

00:38:36.030 --> 00:38:40.350
I have experienced it, even
if just in an imaginary way,

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:44.130
I've had that positive
experience of thinking about,

00:38:44.130 --> 00:38:45.720
well, how great my life is going to be

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:47.430
when I get this thing done,"

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:49.890
and they start to sort
of rest on their laurels.

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:51.750
She's actually measured
systolic blood pressure

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:52.830
and heart rate,

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.200
and they found that people who do that,

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:56.587
who go through that experience of,

00:38:56.587 --> 00:38:58.200
"Visualizing how great my life will be

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:00.570
when I get X, Y and Z done,"

00:39:00.570 --> 00:39:02.310
their systolic blood pressure,

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:06.690
the bottom number on your blood
pressure reading, decreases.

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:09.330
Okay, now, I'm all about
finding ways to relax,

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:10.530
especially in New York, right?

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:13.170
You're constantly living at
a high level of stimulation,

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:14.310
and so like, "Cool, great,

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:15.570
so maybe I should just think about

00:39:15.570 --> 00:39:16.560
how awesome my life will be

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:19.440
when I get my bucket list done,"

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:22.650
but motivation scientists know
that systolic blood pressure

00:39:22.650 --> 00:39:25.020
is actually an indicator
of our body's readiness

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.660
to get up and act, to do something.

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:29.400
Now, that can be the going out for a walk,

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:31.650
going out for a run, hitting the gym,

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:34.100
it can also be things
like doing math problems,

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:34.933
[laughing] right?

00:39:34.933 --> 00:39:36.660
Even if it's something that's just mental,

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:38.970
systolic blood pressure actually goes up

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:42.420
in anticipation of your body or your mind

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:43.770
needing to do something,

00:39:43.770 --> 00:39:46.263
taking the first steps on a goal.

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:50.377
So then it helps us to understand of like,

00:39:50.377 --> 00:39:52.620
"Okay, if I've just
created this dream board,

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:54.780
this vision board, and
put myself psychologically

00:39:54.780 --> 00:39:56.970
in that space of a goal satisfied,

00:39:56.970 --> 00:39:58.740
why is it bad that blood
pressure goes down?"

00:39:58.740 --> 00:40:00.900
Because it means your
body is chilling out,

00:40:00.900 --> 00:40:02.310
it's like, "All right, cool,

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:04.477
I just accomplished something
pretty major," right?

00:40:04.477 --> 00:40:07.770
"I actually now don't have
the physiological resources

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:10.230
at the ready to take
the first step right now

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:11.630
to do something about that."

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:16.140
So that was a pretty monumental finding

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:17.340
for motivation scientists,

00:40:17.340 --> 00:40:19.230
to understand that creating
these dream boards,

00:40:19.230 --> 00:40:20.760
these vision boards, or to-do lists,

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:22.410
might actually backfire,

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:24.750
because it in and of itself

00:40:24.750 --> 00:40:28.260
is the creation of a goal and
the satisfaction of the goal,

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.450
and then people
understandably give themselves

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:33.960
some time to just enjoy
that positive experience.

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:35.670
- So much for "The Secret".

00:40:35.670 --> 00:40:37.620
- [Emily] Yeah, exactly,
[laughing] exactly.

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:39.600
- I guess now "The Secret"
folks will come after me

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:41.190
with pitchforks, but.
- I try to never

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:42.030
say the name, [laughing] right?

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:43.170
For that reason.
- Well, I'm not afraid

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:44.400
to say the name, I mean,

00:40:44.400 --> 00:40:45.930
I imagine that certain strategies

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:46.830
might work for other people,

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:49.140
but everything you're saying, again,

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:50.220
is consistent with what we know

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:53.340
about the physiology of dopamine
circuits for motivation.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:57.600
I have a good friend who perhaps
incidentally, perhaps not,

00:40:57.600 --> 00:41:00.750
is a cardiologist at a major university,

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:04.650
said that one of the major
errors that people make

00:41:04.650 --> 00:41:06.300
with book writing and completion

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:09.300
is they will tell people
they're going to write a book

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:10.387
and people will say,

00:41:10.387 --> 00:41:11.940
"Oh, you definitely should write a book,

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:13.110
everyone's going to love your book,"

00:41:13.110 --> 00:41:14.277
and they never end up writing it.

00:41:14.277 --> 00:41:18.330
And his theory is that they
get so much dopamine reward

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:19.890
from that immediate feedback,

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:22.710
with all the protection of never
having the book criticized,

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:24.120
that they never write the book.

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:25.620
I'm sure there are exceptions to this,

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:28.110
but I guess it raises the question,

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:29.460
what's the better strategy?

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:31.230
- Yeah, so I'm not saying

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:34.050
that people who enjoy dream board creation

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:35.370
should stop what they're doing,

00:41:35.370 --> 00:41:36.840
that's not the take-home message here.

00:41:36.840 --> 00:41:38.160
- Nightmare board.
- [laughing] Oh,

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:39.420
definitely not that, no.
- Okay, okay.

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:41.310
- There's enough anxiety
and fear in the world,

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:43.440
we don't need to encourage more of it.

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:44.640
But the process of goal setting

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:47.140
shouldn't stop with
articulating what the goal is.

00:41:48.180 --> 00:41:50.617
So at that same point that
we're trying to figure out,

00:41:50.617 --> 00:41:51.450
"What do we want to do,

00:41:51.450 --> 00:41:53.940
what is my vision for the future?"

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:55.770
in those planning sessions,

00:41:55.770 --> 00:41:57.960
we need to simultaneously think about

00:41:57.960 --> 00:41:59.340
a couple of other things.

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:02.820
One is how are we going to get there?

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:04.500
So take it out of the abstract,

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:08.220
take it out of this
idyllic visual iconography

00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:10.170
and start thinking about
the practical day to day.

00:42:10.170 --> 00:42:11.940
We need to break it down
into more manageable goals,

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:14.010
not just, "My 10-year plan for myself,"

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:15.480
but, "My two-week plan,

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:17.490
what can I accomplish
in the next two weeks

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:18.780
and the two weeks after

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:21.210
that's going to set me
on the right trajectory?"

00:42:21.210 --> 00:42:22.140
That's probably not surprising

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:23.977
to anybody [laughing]
who's been thinking about,

00:42:23.977 --> 00:42:25.497
"How do I set goals better?"

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:29.250
Plan big picture, think
big picture abstractly,

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:31.680
but then also break it
down more concretely,

00:42:31.680 --> 00:42:32.790
that's probably not surprising,

00:42:32.790 --> 00:42:36.960
but it's an important aspect
of the goal setting process.

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:39.000
Then, again, Gabriele
Oettingen in my department

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:41.640
has identified a third often overlooked

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:44.890
or underappreciated
stage that has to happen

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:47.520
in the goal setting process,

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:49.230
and that's thinking about the obstacles

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:52.080
that stand in your way of success,

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:54.900
and that, it will actually
help improve motivation

00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:56.190
in the long run.

00:42:56.190 --> 00:42:58.897
And sometimes people think
that that is counterintuitive,

00:42:58.897 --> 00:43:01.620
"You're saying if I want
to increase my motivation,

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:02.460
have more motivation,

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:04.200
then I need to think about
how hard it's going to be,

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:05.940
all the ways that I'm going to fail?

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:07.860
How is that going to jazz me up?

00:43:07.860 --> 00:43:12.860
How's that going to help me get
through when things get hard?"

00:43:13.500 --> 00:43:17.490
But it does because it's
like coming up with a plan B,

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:19.170
a plan C, plan D,

00:43:19.170 --> 00:43:21.660
in advance of actually experiencing that.

00:43:21.660 --> 00:43:24.030
If you were on a boat and
the boat started to sink,

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:25.020
that's not the time

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:27.030
you want to start
looking for life jackets.

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:28.140
You already want to know where one is

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:30.120
so you can go to it right away.

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:32.340
And it's the same thing with goal setting,

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:34.260
is that you want to know,
"What am I working towards?

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:35.160
How am I going to get there?

00:43:35.160 --> 00:43:37.440
And if I experience this obstacle,

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:39.060
here's what I'm going to do about it."

00:43:39.060 --> 00:43:41.040
You may never experience that obstacle,

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:43.860
but if you do, you're probably
going to be shy on time,

00:43:43.860 --> 00:43:45.300
thin on resources,

00:43:45.300 --> 00:43:47.730
maybe experiencing an anxiety
that hijacks your brain

00:43:47.730 --> 00:43:48.990
so you're not functioning

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:52.650
at that optimal level of
judgment and decision making.

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:55.800
You want to already have
the snap next step in place

00:43:55.800 --> 00:43:57.390
so you can just hop to it, right?

00:43:57.390 --> 00:43:59.220
We're not going to do our best thinking

00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:00.753
when we're in crisis mode,

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:05.100
but we don't have to
if we have already used

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:06.660
our resources in advance to come up with

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:08.640
that plan B or that plan C.

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:11.160
Michael Phelps, incredible athlete, right?

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:12.630
This is something that he and his coach

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:16.590
have routinely incorporated
into their training.

00:44:16.590 --> 00:44:19.500
So I love this story that, back in 2008,

00:44:19.500 --> 00:44:23.190
he was hot for the first time
on the international stage.

00:44:23.190 --> 00:44:24.360
It was the Beijing Olympics,

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:26.100
Michael Phelps was on the
brink of doing something

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:28.590
that no one else in the
history of the Olympic Games

00:44:28.590 --> 00:44:29.423
has ever done,

00:44:29.423 --> 00:44:32.790
which was win eight gold
medals in a single Olympiad.

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:34.110
At the time of this story,

00:44:34.110 --> 00:44:35.640
he had already won seven

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:37.800
and he had just the
200 fly in front of him

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:39.897
before he could do what
no one else has ever done,

00:44:39.897 --> 00:44:41.970
win the eighth gold medal.

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:44.760
And the fly is his thing, right?

00:44:44.760 --> 00:44:46.410
This should've been easy, a no-brainer,

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:49.290
he's going to win this, he's
going to break Olympic history.

00:44:49.290 --> 00:44:51.480
As soon as he dove into the pool,

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:53.280
his goggles started to leak.

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:56.340
And by the time he had done
three lengths of the pool,

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:57.450
he just had to flip around

00:44:57.450 --> 00:45:00.840
and come back to the starting
line slash finish line,

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:01.833
back to the edge.

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:03.990
By the time that happened,

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:05.520
his goggles were completely
filled with water

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:07.230
and he was swimming blind.

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:08.063
I would've panicked,

00:45:08.063 --> 00:45:09.720
I would've sunk to the bottom of the pool,

00:45:09.720 --> 00:45:11.490
I wouldn't have even been
in the pool to be honest,

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:14.565
I'm not a swimmer, definitely
not going to be in the Olympics,

00:45:14.565 --> 00:45:16.710
but for him, he didn't, it
wasn't a moment of panic,

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:17.610
like it probably would've been

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:20.430
for nearly every other
person in that situation,

00:45:20.430 --> 00:45:24.060
because he had foreshadowed
that kind of possible failure.

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:26.850
He had imagined that obstacle
hitting him in advance,

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:28.867
and not even just imagined
it, but practiced it,

00:45:28.867 --> 00:45:30.060
"What will we do?"

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:32.490
He routinely practiced
swimming with his goggles

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:34.110
not fully secured on his face,

00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:37.980
his coach notoriously would rip
the goggles off of his head,

00:45:37.980 --> 00:45:38.850
smash 'em on the ground

00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:40.410
for maybe dramatic effect or something,

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:41.880
so that he didn't even have any goggles

00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:45.060
possible to grab as he's in practice.

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:47.490
So because he had
foreshadowed that possibility

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:50.610
and the solution, "If my
goggles start to leak,

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:52.867
then I will do," in his case,

00:45:52.867 --> 00:45:54.840
"start counting my strokes,

00:45:54.840 --> 00:45:55.770
then I'll make it through."

00:45:55.770 --> 00:45:57.840
He knew exactly how many
strokes it would take from him

00:45:57.840 --> 00:45:59.550
to get from one end of
the pool to the other,

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:01.150
he started counting his strokes.

00:46:02.216 --> 00:46:03.630
He won that race, the 200 fly,

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:04.530
he won his eighth gold medal

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:07.260
and he'd go on to win
15 more in his career.

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:09.060
So we might not all be swimmers,

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:11.970
we might not all aspire to
Olympic-level performance,

00:46:11.970 --> 00:46:13.050
but I love that example

00:46:13.050 --> 00:46:16.290
because I think it helps sort of demystify

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:19.710
or give us an alternative
perspective on the importance

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:21.540
and the motivational reasons

00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:23.970
why thinking about obstacles in advance,

00:46:23.970 --> 00:46:26.760
thinking about the ways,
the two, three, four ways

00:46:26.760 --> 00:46:28.680
that your plan might go awry

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:31.440
is actually effective at helping
us to overcome the obstacle

00:46:31.440 --> 00:46:34.380
that might otherwise lead
us to throw in the towel.

00:46:34.380 --> 00:46:36.960
- That's a beautiful example.

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:39.360
I'm going to springboard off that example

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:42.810
to ask a question that
has also been on my mind,

00:46:42.810 --> 00:46:46.080
which is, is there really
anything special about vision?

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:47.700
Because in the example you just gave,

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:50.970
it was indeed vision that
Michael Phelps was deprived of

00:46:50.970 --> 00:46:53.940
and it was counting strokes.

00:46:53.940 --> 00:46:56.970
Counting is another form
of incremental measurement,

00:46:56.970 --> 00:46:58.863
in the nervous system, obviously.

00:47:00.240 --> 00:47:02.940
There are others, they
could be the sensation

00:47:02.940 --> 00:47:04.680
of the hands smacking the water

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:05.940
or breaking the surface of the water,

00:47:05.940 --> 00:47:08.850
so there are any number
of different variables

00:47:08.850 --> 00:47:11.280
or metrics that one could use.

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:15.030
I could imagine that setting out on a,

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:16.710
let's say, a three-mile run,

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:18.780
which, for me, is a decent-distanced run,

00:47:18.780 --> 00:47:20.100
it's one I do a few times a week,

00:47:20.100 --> 00:47:21.090
I'm also not a runner,

00:47:21.090 --> 00:47:23.190
but I try and complete some
runs a few times a week,

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:24.450
at very slow pace,

00:47:24.450 --> 00:47:26.580
just for my health.
[Emily laughing]

00:47:26.580 --> 00:47:28.740
I could count every step,

00:47:28.740 --> 00:47:30.390
that would be kind of exhausting,

00:47:31.230 --> 00:47:34.020
but if I knew that three miles was,

00:47:34.020 --> 00:47:35.250
I'm going to estimate here, I don't know,

00:47:35.250 --> 00:47:37.710
a couple thousand steps,

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:39.690
I could count backward,
I could count forward,

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:40.893
I could count every 10.

00:47:42.210 --> 00:47:43.740
I confess, I spend every morning

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:45.480
trying to find sunlight
to get sun in my eyes

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:46.680
to set my circadian rhythm,

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:48.390
and I do 100 jumping jacks.

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:50.037
So I'm the guy that people

00:47:50.037 --> 00:47:51.593
are looking at strange on the street.

00:47:51.593 --> 00:47:52.710
[Emily laughing]
But sometimes I count

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:54.180
every 10, sometimes I count backwards,

00:47:54.180 --> 00:47:55.950
sometimes I count forward.

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:57.630
Is there any indication that it matters

00:47:57.630 --> 00:48:01.440
or is it simply that we
attach some sort of meaning

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:06.440
to that increment and the mode
of reaching that increment?

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:08.190
Because it does seem like

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:09.537
there's something special about vision,

00:48:09.537 --> 00:48:12.450
and we could maybe dive into
a little bit more why that is,

00:48:12.450 --> 00:48:14.223
but at a very basic level,

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:18.990
how broadly or finally should
one set the increments,

00:48:18.990 --> 00:48:21.090
and does it matter if
you're counting steps

00:48:21.090 --> 00:48:22.263
or counting strokes,

00:48:23.100 --> 00:48:25.110
maybe it's every other song,

00:48:25.110 --> 00:48:27.270
you're going to listen to an entire album.

00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:28.785
That's something that I don't
know if people do anymore.

00:48:28.785 --> 00:48:29.850
[Emily laughing]
Or you're going to listen

00:48:29.850 --> 00:48:30.870
to a whole playlist,

00:48:30.870 --> 00:48:32.160
and then listen to it again,

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:34.620
and you're going to run
as long as the playlist

00:48:34.620 --> 00:48:37.050
is completed twice,

00:48:37.050 --> 00:48:38.760
you can obviously see what I'm getting at,

00:48:38.760 --> 00:48:40.663
but I know people are going to
want to implement these tools.

00:48:40.663 --> 00:48:42.750
And I have to guess
that the nervous system

00:48:42.750 --> 00:48:46.290
is somewhat indiscriminent
when it comes to these things,

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:49.020
but that there might
also be some specificity.

00:48:49.020 --> 00:48:52.373
- I think vision is special,
and I think you do too,

00:48:53.370 --> 00:48:54.930
and for a variety of reasons.

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:56.490
When you start, you can really nerd out

00:48:56.490 --> 00:48:58.080
on how cool the brain is

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:00.150
and how cool vision is within the brain.

00:49:00.150 --> 00:49:01.860
And when you do, then you
start to find some things

00:49:01.860 --> 00:49:04.080
that make vision unique, right?

00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:08.070
More real estate, more
neurological cortex real estate

00:49:08.070 --> 00:49:11.010
is taken up by the visual
sense than any other sense,

00:49:11.010 --> 00:49:14.580
more than taste, touch, smell, right?

00:49:14.580 --> 00:49:16.350
Vision gets more real estate,

00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:18.600
gets more neurological processing space

00:49:18.600 --> 00:49:20.370
than any other sense, why is that?

00:49:20.370 --> 00:49:21.960
Well, because evolution has led us

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:25.770
to prioritize the visual experience.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:27.240
There's some cool illusions

00:49:27.240 --> 00:49:28.650
where maybe somebody's mouth

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:31.020
is doing something different
than what you're hearing,

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:34.410
when people sort of
create these weird tricks

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:36.543
that might go on YouTube and go viral,

00:49:37.440 --> 00:49:39.420
and people are trying to
figure out, "What did I hear?

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:41.490
What did I see his mouth doing?"

00:49:41.490 --> 00:49:44.190
And what comes up is
that people prioritize

00:49:44.190 --> 00:49:45.810
what they see over what they're hearing

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:48.630
when the two are incompatible
or kind of out of sync.

00:49:48.630 --> 00:49:50.670
- Every time.
- Yeah, every time, right?

00:49:50.670 --> 00:49:52.260
If you have to bet on it,

00:49:52.260 --> 00:49:53.610
bet on what it is that you're looking at

00:49:53.610 --> 00:49:55.350
rather than what you're seeing.

00:49:55.350 --> 00:49:56.700
And why is that?

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:58.500
Well, [sighing] I guess, a
couple other things too, right?

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:01.710
We can see super far, you
can see a flickering candle

00:50:01.710 --> 00:50:05.940
on a horizon if it was a totally clear sky

00:50:05.940 --> 00:50:06.930
several miles away.

00:50:06.930 --> 00:50:08.700
You can see the
International Space Station

00:50:08.700 --> 00:50:11.250
floating up in the night sky, right?

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:12.870
Hundreds of miles away,

00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:14.493
our eyes are amazing.

00:50:16.303 --> 00:50:18.393
And we prioritize what we see,

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:21.390
and I think that's because we never,

00:50:21.390 --> 00:50:23.250
we rarely [laughing] get the experience

00:50:23.250 --> 00:50:25.600
of having our visual
experience second guessed.

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:28.200
Oftentimes, we're having a conversation

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:29.370
maybe in a loud restaurant

00:50:29.370 --> 00:50:30.657
and we know that we didn't
hear the person, right?

00:50:30.657 --> 00:50:32.180
And so we say like,
"Oh, did you say that?"

00:50:32.180 --> 00:50:33.750
Or like, "Oh, I thought you said this,"

00:50:33.750 --> 00:50:34.950
and they're like, "No, I
didn't say that," right?

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:39.390
So people will correct us
when our ears get it wrong,

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:41.610
or we're tasting something amazing

00:50:41.610 --> 00:50:43.950
and we can't quite figure
out what spices were in here,

00:50:43.950 --> 00:50:45.570
and so we know that our tongue

00:50:45.570 --> 00:50:48.750
isn't quite picking up
the taste the right way,

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:49.890
and that's why we read the menu

00:50:49.890 --> 00:50:51.870
to see what are the
ingredients or we ask the chef,

00:50:51.870 --> 00:50:52.703
like, "What did you put in this?

00:50:52.703 --> 00:50:54.600
It tastes amazing."

00:50:54.600 --> 00:50:56.220
So we know that our tongue
is getting it wrong,

00:50:56.220 --> 00:50:57.900
or you might be touching something

00:50:57.900 --> 00:51:00.150
and you look at the tag to
see what sort of textile

00:51:00.150 --> 00:51:02.220
was used in this really
amazing piece of clothing

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:03.450
that you're looking to buy.

00:51:03.450 --> 00:51:05.400
So we know that our sense of touch

00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:06.720
isn't quite getting it right.

00:51:06.720 --> 00:51:08.370
But rarely do we have that experience

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:11.340
of having our eyes get updated,

00:51:11.340 --> 00:51:12.173
where we're looking at something,

00:51:12.173 --> 00:51:13.590
"Oh, I think I'm looking at my mom.

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:15.900
Oh, no, actually, it was
actually my husband."

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:17.910
Okay, that never happens,
[laughing] right?

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:19.620
That we have gotten vision as wrong

00:51:19.620 --> 00:51:21.630
as we might get any other thing

00:51:21.630 --> 00:51:24.810
that we're experiencing
through any other sense.

00:51:24.810 --> 00:51:26.880
We trust our visual experience,

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:28.890
we have a sort of a naive realism

00:51:28.890 --> 00:51:32.850
that what we see reflects the
world the way it actually is,

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:35.280
because it's never really fully tested,

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:37.920
we never get the input or the feedback

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:40.110
that you've seen something wrong,

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:42.483
until a visual illusion pops
up on social media, right?

00:51:42.483 --> 00:51:44.400
Like the dress example,

00:51:44.400 --> 00:51:46.140
or the last week or so,

00:51:46.140 --> 00:51:48.720
there's been that horse-seal-lion drawing

00:51:48.720 --> 00:51:50.707
that's been all over social
media too, "What do you see?"

00:51:50.707 --> 00:51:52.410
"I see a horse," someone
says, "I see a seal,"

00:51:52.410 --> 00:51:54.150
and then chaos erupts,

00:51:54.150 --> 00:51:56.107
or, "I thought the dress was blue,"

00:51:56.107 --> 00:51:57.840
"No, I thought it was gold."

00:51:57.840 --> 00:51:59.700
I don't remember the options
'cause I see it as blue,

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:01.890
so, [laughing] right?
[Andrew laughing]

00:52:01.890 --> 00:52:04.170
And it's dividing up
families and friendships

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:05.580
because you've seen something

00:52:05.580 --> 00:52:08.100
that the other person
just literally cannot see.

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:10.350
And that's why we love those examples

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:13.140
when they pop up in
social media when they do,

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:16.503
is because it defies all of
our previous expectations.

00:52:18.249 --> 00:52:19.800
If this interests you,

00:52:19.800 --> 00:52:22.110
there's a really amazing
visual artist, Anish Kapoor,

00:52:22.110 --> 00:52:23.640
who plays with these ideas too,

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:26.940
and his installations
are just fascinating.

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:29.220
I saw one at a museum once,

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:31.380
where I walked down this long hall

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.240
and it's just a big black rectangle

00:52:33.240 --> 00:52:34.818
that's painted on the wall.

00:52:34.818 --> 00:52:36.960
And I was like, "This guy's
super famous, what the hell?

00:52:36.960 --> 00:52:40.230
It's just a big black
rectangle painted on the wall.

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:42.810
What is this about,
what a hoax? [laughing]

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:45.510
This museum paid how
much, what?" or whatever.

00:52:45.510 --> 00:52:46.980
But then as you get
closer, you get closer,

00:52:46.980 --> 00:52:48.570
and your eyes start to settle in

00:52:48.570 --> 00:52:50.340
and they adapt to the
different visual lighting,

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:52.290
you realize it's not a black
square painted on the wall,

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:54.510
it's a huge hole he's
carved into the wall,

00:52:54.510 --> 00:52:56.310
and there is a whole other world

00:52:56.310 --> 00:52:59.250
that's back behind there that
you can't see right away,

00:52:59.250 --> 00:53:02.280
until your eyes adapt to the
different lighting conditions.

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:03.510
- Beautiful.
- It's amazing,

00:53:03.510 --> 00:53:04.500
yeah.
- As a vision scientist,

00:53:04.500 --> 00:53:06.780
I have to see, where is this exhibit?

00:53:06.780 --> 00:53:08.043
- It's not up right now,

00:53:09.270 --> 00:53:11.010
there was a retrospective
several years ago

00:53:11.010 --> 00:53:13.380
that was done in Sydney,

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:14.267
but his work is all over the place.

00:53:14.267 --> 00:53:16.140
- Great.
- So Anish Kapoor,

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:18.584
definitely worth looking up,

00:53:18.584 --> 00:53:20.370
because, like the dress example,

00:53:20.370 --> 00:53:21.780
or the horse-seal-lion drawing,

00:53:21.780 --> 00:53:24.240
or artists like Anish Kapoor's work,

00:53:24.240 --> 00:53:26.580
that is a moment that gives us

00:53:26.580 --> 00:53:30.180
a different unexpected
insight about the world.

00:53:30.180 --> 00:53:32.520
That it challenges us to see something

00:53:32.520 --> 00:53:33.660
that we hadn't seen before,

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:35.970
or it induces, or tricks
us into seeing something

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:38.550
that we wouldn't have otherwise have seen.

00:53:38.550 --> 00:53:39.900
And so it's those rare moments

00:53:39.900 --> 00:53:41.790
that I think are actually really important

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:44.850
for understanding what
do our eyes normally do?

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:47.550
Because we wouldn't find
these examples so surprising,

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:49.110
so engaging, so shocking,

00:53:49.110 --> 00:53:50.970
if we had routinely gotten the experience

00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:53.610
of realizing we're not seeing
the world the way that it is.

00:53:53.610 --> 00:53:57.750
So that is why I think vision is special,

00:53:57.750 --> 00:54:00.330
and why it can be thought of as a tool

00:54:00.330 --> 00:54:02.610
that we can add to our toolkit

00:54:02.610 --> 00:54:05.010
for how to better accomplish our goals.

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:07.410
I'm not saying that we
should just only focus on

00:54:07.410 --> 00:54:10.860
imagining the world through
an attentional spotlight,

00:54:10.860 --> 00:54:14.070
but maybe that's something that
we can employ strategically

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:16.800
on occasion when we think
it's going to best help us,

00:54:16.800 --> 00:54:19.373
when we need an extra little push to cross

00:54:19.373 --> 00:54:21.720
that literal or metaphorical finish line,

00:54:21.720 --> 00:54:24.630
but it doesn't have to be
the only tactic that we use,

00:54:24.630 --> 00:54:26.460
just like it's not bad
to use vision boards,

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:28.173
but let's use something else also.

00:54:29.215 --> 00:54:31.470
It's not bad to talk to
ourselves in encouraging ways,

00:54:31.470 --> 00:54:35.370
but let's try adding another
tool to our tool belt

00:54:35.370 --> 00:54:38.670
in case that's not enough
to get the job done.

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:40.440
So I do think that there's great power

00:54:40.440 --> 00:54:42.360
in thinking about our visual experience

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:45.030
alongside other tactics that we might use

00:54:45.030 --> 00:54:46.500
for meeting our goals.

00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:47.580
And another one of those tactics

00:54:47.580 --> 00:54:50.737
might be like the numerics
that you're talking about,

00:54:50.737 --> 00:54:53.850
"Do I think about my jumping
jacks in terms of groups of 10

00:54:53.850 --> 00:54:55.980
or as a set of 100?"

00:54:55.980 --> 00:54:57.090
You do it routinely,

00:54:57.090 --> 00:54:59.100
so you might be able to set a goal of 100

00:54:59.100 --> 00:55:03.060
and have that sustain you
through number 60, number 70,

00:55:03.060 --> 00:55:04.890
when maybe it's starting to get harder,

00:55:04.890 --> 00:55:06.390
but for somebody who's just starting out

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:08.130
and wants to be able to make it to 100,

00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:09.680
that's probably not going to work,

00:55:10.760 --> 00:55:12.000
that could be quite challenging for them

00:55:12.000 --> 00:55:13.920
if it's the first time
that they're trying it.

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:17.700
And so instead, setting those
micro goals of groups of 10

00:55:17.700 --> 00:55:19.080
is going to be useful,

00:55:19.080 --> 00:55:21.420
because as we start to get
to number eight or nine,

00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:25.140
or number 88 or 89, and
it's really getting hard,

00:55:25.140 --> 00:55:27.690
we need that extra little hedonic hit

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:29.580
of pleasure of accomplishment,

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:31.170
the micro dopamine rush

00:55:31.170 --> 00:55:34.990
that you might get by hitting
another decade milestone,

00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:39.000
another group-of-10 milestone.

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:40.680
And once we get that
little hit of pleasure,

00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:43.170
excitement or self-congratulations,

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:44.610
that might be enough to sustain us

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:47.130
through the next challenging
physical obstacle,

00:55:47.130 --> 00:55:49.770
the next group of 10
that we might experience.

00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:52.440
So there isn't any
prescription that I would give

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:54.270
and say, "Every person should decide

00:55:54.270 --> 00:55:56.880
that 25 jumping jacks is the goal."

00:55:56.880 --> 00:55:58.770
No, we have to be idiosyncratic

00:55:58.770 --> 00:56:01.590
and introspect about, "Where
are we at with this goal,

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:03.420
this thing that I'm trying to accomplish?"

00:56:03.420 --> 00:56:06.480
and set those goals realistically,

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:08.430
but inspirationally as well.

00:56:08.430 --> 00:56:10.710
We want to set a goal
that will challenge us,

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:12.540
but isn't impossible.

00:56:12.540 --> 00:56:14.550
We don't want to set
goals that are too easy,

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:16.290
because we're not going to trick ourselves

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:20.850
into feeling so great about
doing one jumping jack, okay?

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:23.190
Great, I'm pretty sure most people,

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:25.140
if that's a goal, they can do one,

00:56:25.140 --> 00:56:27.390
so are you going to feel so
great when you hit that goal?

00:56:27.390 --> 00:56:29.010
No, because it was too easy,

00:56:29.010 --> 00:56:32.130
you didn't have any doubt
that you could do that one,

00:56:32.130 --> 00:56:33.603
but what about 25?

00:56:34.590 --> 00:56:37.260
Okay, yeah, I might feel
pretty good about that.

00:56:37.260 --> 00:56:38.427
Well, what about the next group of 25

00:56:38.427 --> 00:56:40.020
and now I'm at 50?

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:41.250
Those are goals that might seem

00:56:41.250 --> 00:56:42.810
just beyond the brink of what's possible,

00:56:42.810 --> 00:56:44.790
but I will feel good when I hit that,

00:56:44.790 --> 00:56:47.220
and that's going to give me the
next sort of boost of energy

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:48.900
that I'm going to need to
go a little bit further,

00:56:48.900 --> 00:56:51.240
either that time or the next time.

00:56:51.240 --> 00:56:53.130
- Yeah, I think vision is special,

00:56:53.130 --> 00:56:55.830
again, I'm strongly biased here.

00:56:55.830 --> 00:57:00.780
The reason I initially
learned about your work was,

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:02.220
well, now you have this amazing book,

00:57:02.220 --> 00:57:04.050
but at the time, there wasn't the book,

00:57:04.050 --> 00:57:06.030
there were just the scientific papers,

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:08.853
and of course, upon which the book rests,

00:57:09.810 --> 00:57:12.540
and those papers are really important,

00:57:12.540 --> 00:57:15.210
but was the relationship between vision

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:16.980
and obviously is our sense of space,

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:19.320
but how the sense of space
and time are related.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:23.700
And to make the idea quite
simple for those listening,

00:57:23.700 --> 00:57:27.300
when you narrow your visual
window, you're measuring,

00:57:27.300 --> 00:57:29.730
the time bin also gets smaller, right?

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:31.170
Which makes sense when you hear it,

00:57:31.170 --> 00:57:33.900
whereas if you take on
a huge visual landscape,

00:57:33.900 --> 00:57:35.490
you're actually carving
up time differently.

00:57:35.490 --> 00:57:38.790
It's sort of like moving
from a slow frame rate

00:57:38.790 --> 00:57:40.590
to a fine frame rate,

00:57:40.590 --> 00:57:42.870
slow-motion camera is actually taking

00:57:42.870 --> 00:57:44.460
a lot more snapshots, right?

00:57:44.460 --> 00:57:46.860
So you're measuring distance
over time more finely.

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:49.590
And so, whereas strobe
would be the other example,

00:57:49.590 --> 00:57:51.840
which a strobe is very low frequency,

00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:53.310
so you're going here, here, here,

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:55.200
as opposed to slow motion, right?

00:57:55.200 --> 00:58:00.200
Strobe gives a course
view into the time domain

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:02.430
and high-speed photography
gives a fine view

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:03.480
into the time domain.

00:58:03.480 --> 00:58:04.860
So I'm almost certain,

00:58:04.860 --> 00:58:07.773
without any knowledge of underlying data,

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:10.800
but knowledge of the mechanism,

00:58:10.800 --> 00:58:14.010
that I'm almost certain, if not certain,

00:58:14.010 --> 00:58:17.250
that by placing a narrow visual aperture,

00:58:17.250 --> 00:58:19.680
we change the way we perceive time.

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:22.140
Now, I have a question, and to be honest,

00:58:22.140 --> 00:58:23.280
I know the answer in advance,

00:58:23.280 --> 00:58:25.020
but I'd love for you to tell us

00:58:25.020 --> 00:58:30.020
a bit about how some of this
works still further in reverse,

00:58:31.080 --> 00:58:34.530
meaning how unfit people view the world

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:36.540
versus how fit people view the world,

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:40.230
or how unmotivated people
visually see the world,

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:43.170
as opposed to highly motivated people.

00:58:43.170 --> 00:58:45.270
You talked about these elite runners,

00:58:45.270 --> 00:58:46.830
you gave them Michael Phelps's example,

00:58:46.830 --> 00:58:49.320
but maybe you could describe that study,

00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:51.390
I think it's a particularly important one,

00:58:51.390 --> 00:58:54.840
mostly because, yes, it identifies

00:58:54.840 --> 00:58:57.150
perhaps the physiological
or psychological differences

00:58:57.150 --> 00:58:58.650
between motivated and unmotivated,

00:58:58.650 --> 00:59:01.080
or fit and unfit people,

00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:04.770
but it also provides
a path to remedy that.

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:08.670
- Mm-hmm, yeah, out of my lab,

00:59:08.670 --> 00:59:10.470
but also out of several other labs,

00:59:10.470 --> 00:59:12.720
there's been work looking at that relation

00:59:12.720 --> 00:59:16.170
between states of the body
and visual experiences.

00:59:16.170 --> 00:59:19.230
They haven't necessarily
tried to integrate

00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:21.750
the motivation science element to it,

00:59:21.750 --> 00:59:23.000
but they were looking to see

00:59:23.000 --> 00:59:25.080
do visual experiences change

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:27.090
as a function of different
states of our body?

00:59:27.090 --> 00:59:30.270
So they've looked at people
who experience chronic fatigue,

00:59:30.270 --> 00:59:32.403
the elderly, people who are overweight,

00:59:33.240 --> 00:59:36.840
those that are wearing heavy backpacks,

00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:39.000
and so who are sort of
put into that experience

00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:40.110
of being overweight,

00:59:40.110 --> 00:59:42.540
what happens to their
perceptions of the environment?

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:45.390
Well, what they find is
that distances look further

00:59:45.390 --> 00:59:47.190
to those that are overweight,
chronically tired,

00:59:47.190 --> 00:59:48.390
older rather than younger,

00:59:48.390 --> 00:59:50.583
weighted down with extra baggage,

00:59:51.690 --> 00:59:54.780
distances look farther
and hills look steeper.

00:59:54.780 --> 00:59:56.880
We've done some of those studies too,

00:59:56.880 --> 00:59:59.370
where we try to give people more energy,

00:59:59.370 --> 01:00:01.920
or deprive them of energy,

01:00:01.920 --> 01:00:04.980
and see does that change
their perception of space.

01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:09.270
And we did that by sort
of a classic technique

01:00:09.270 --> 01:00:10.920
of a double-blind study

01:00:10.920 --> 01:00:12.420
where the participant doesn't really know

01:00:12.420 --> 01:00:13.770
what they're experiencing.

01:00:13.770 --> 01:00:15.565
- I thought you were going
to say, "A double espresso."

01:00:15.565 --> 01:00:17.056
- Oh. [laughing]

01:00:17.056 --> 01:00:21.003
That is also a good psychological
experience to give people.

01:00:22.200 --> 01:00:24.510
Yeah, so a double-blind experiment

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:27.150
where the participant doesn't
really know the full extent

01:00:27.150 --> 01:00:29.400
of what they're doing or
what they're experiencing,

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:32.430
and the researcher who's
interacting with them also doesn't.

01:00:32.430 --> 01:00:34.290
They do this a lot in medical studies,

01:00:34.290 --> 01:00:35.820
you give somebody a drug

01:00:35.820 --> 01:00:38.640
and you give somebody a
placebo, a sugar pill,

01:00:38.640 --> 01:00:41.250
and then importantly, nobody really knows

01:00:41.250 --> 01:00:43.740
who's got what until you've
analyzed all the data

01:00:43.740 --> 01:00:44.730
and the results are revealed

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:46.950
that these are the
people that had the drug,

01:00:46.950 --> 01:00:48.270
the active agent,

01:00:48.270 --> 01:00:50.340
same idea in the psychological research.

01:00:50.340 --> 01:00:53.220
In this case, what we did was
give people Kool-Aid to drink.

01:00:53.220 --> 01:00:56.490
And for some people, that
Kool-Aid was sweetened with sugar,

01:00:56.490 --> 01:01:00.780
an actual caloric entity,
it could give them energy.

01:01:00.780 --> 01:01:04.110
Other people drank Kool-Aid
sweetened with Splenda.

01:01:04.110 --> 01:01:05.280
So, yeah, it's sweet,

01:01:05.280 --> 01:01:06.930
but it actually doesn't
have any caloric value,

01:01:06.930 --> 01:01:08.400
you're not giving people energy,

01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:12.660
you're just giving them that
experience of sweetness.

01:01:12.660 --> 01:01:13.500
Now, some people, of course,

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:15.360
are really good at identifying

01:01:15.360 --> 01:01:17.070
what's real sugar and what's Splenda,

01:01:17.070 --> 01:01:20.370
but when you put it into
Kool-Aid, a pretty noxious powder,

01:01:20.370 --> 01:01:22.050
it actually masked it for everybody

01:01:22.050 --> 01:01:23.460
and nobody had any idea.

01:01:23.460 --> 01:01:24.424
- 'Cause it tasted like garbage

01:01:24.424 --> 01:01:25.890
to everybody.
- It tastes like garbage,

01:01:25.890 --> 01:01:26.880
yeah. [laughing]
- Sorry, Kool-Aid.

01:01:26.880 --> 01:01:29.010
I mean, I'm sure there are
many people that love Kool-Aid,

01:01:29.010 --> 01:01:31.993
I guess the sales of Kool-Aid
will reveal the data.

01:01:31.993 --> 01:01:33.750
- [laughing] Yeah, I grew
up in Nebraska actually,

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:35.040
where Kool-Aid is from,

01:01:35.040 --> 01:01:36.260
it originated in Nebraska.
- Oh.

01:01:36.260 --> 01:01:39.425
- So I do feel like I'm
betraying my roots slightly

01:01:39.425 --> 01:01:41.125
by casting some shade on Kool-Aid,

01:01:42.660 --> 01:01:43.650
but that's how it worked,

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:45.810
is that we asked them
to guess what they got,

01:01:45.810 --> 01:01:47.850
we tested them afterwards
and they were wrong.

01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:50.887
So nobody's able to guess with accuracy,

01:01:50.887 --> 01:01:52.740
"What was your drink sweetened with?"

01:01:52.740 --> 01:01:55.770
Which is important
because they were blind,

01:01:55.770 --> 01:01:57.270
the way that scientists use it,

01:01:57.270 --> 01:01:59.920
they didn't know what it
was that they were drinking.

01:02:02.057 --> 01:02:03.510
We give them about 10 to 15 minutes

01:02:03.510 --> 01:02:05.040
for that sugar to metabolize,

01:02:05.040 --> 01:02:08.310
and we measured their
circulating blood glucose levels

01:02:08.310 --> 01:02:10.290
to make sure that we had, in fact,

01:02:10.290 --> 01:02:14.280
given their body circulating glucose,

01:02:14.280 --> 01:02:17.253
energy that they might
use in the next activity.

01:02:18.875 --> 01:02:19.708
And the researcher, again,

01:02:19.708 --> 01:02:23.490
didn't know whether they had
just served sugar or Splenda,

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:25.650
then we asked people to estimate distance.

01:02:25.650 --> 01:02:27.210
So we gave some people more energy,

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:28.860
or we kept others sort of at

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:30.900
whatever their normal level was,

01:02:30.900 --> 01:02:32.160
and what we found is that those people

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:33.120
who didn't even know it

01:02:33.120 --> 01:02:34.830
but who had been given more energy

01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:37.650
by drinking Kool-Aid sweetened with sugar,

01:02:37.650 --> 01:02:40.800
perceived their space as more constricted,

01:02:40.800 --> 01:02:43.350
that visual illusion of
proximity was induced,

01:02:43.350 --> 01:02:44.820
they felt that their finish line,

01:02:44.820 --> 01:02:47.340
again, in the context of an exercise task,

01:02:47.340 --> 01:02:48.990
was closer to them.

01:02:48.990 --> 01:02:51.810
So in just the same way that
these other physiology labs,

01:02:51.810 --> 01:02:53.370
vision science, physiology labs,

01:02:53.370 --> 01:02:55.710
found that people who
are chronically tired,

01:02:55.710 --> 01:02:58.410
who don't feel like they
have as much energy,

01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:00.090
or those that are physically weighted down

01:03:00.090 --> 01:03:03.693
and for whom moving within an
environment is more costly,

01:03:04.530 --> 01:03:06.420
we could create that
experience for people.

01:03:06.420 --> 01:03:08.460
We did an experimental version of that,

01:03:08.460 --> 01:03:12.120
that if you have more energy,
the world looks easier,

01:03:12.120 --> 01:03:15.363
the distances to a finish
line don't look as far.

01:03:16.320 --> 01:03:19.230
So that was some of the
experimental evidence that we had

01:03:19.230 --> 01:03:20.700
to show that people's states of their body

01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:22.680
do impact their visual experience.

01:03:22.680 --> 01:03:24.660
Now, I'm a motivation researcher,

01:03:24.660 --> 01:03:26.040
so for me, the big question is,

01:03:26.040 --> 01:03:28.080
well, what's the point of that study then,

01:03:28.080 --> 01:03:30.600
besides just showing this
connection between the body

01:03:30.600 --> 01:03:32.910
and the eyes and the visual experience?

01:03:32.910 --> 01:03:35.640
We think that that's fundamental
to one of the reasons

01:03:35.640 --> 01:03:38.340
that people experience difficulty
when they're exercising,

01:03:38.340 --> 01:03:40.500
when it's really harder for your body,

01:03:40.500 --> 01:03:44.283
because of its physical
state, to move within a space,

01:03:46.350 --> 01:03:48.420
you might say like, "Well, why
don't they just go exercise?"

01:03:48.420 --> 01:03:50.640
Because the world looks harder to them,

01:03:50.640 --> 01:03:53.520
because that distance that
they're supposed to walk

01:03:53.520 --> 01:03:54.960
because a doctor tells them to,

01:03:54.960 --> 01:03:56.910
or that a partner encourages them to,

01:03:56.910 --> 01:03:58.920
or a hill that they should hike up

01:03:58.920 --> 01:03:59.760
because someone told them

01:03:59.760 --> 01:04:00.990
that would be good for their health,

01:04:00.990 --> 01:04:03.570
it looks more challenging to them

01:04:03.570 --> 01:04:08.570
than it does to somebody who's
in better physical health.

01:04:08.760 --> 01:04:11.610
Now, if it looks that
way, if it looks harder,

01:04:11.610 --> 01:04:14.310
if it feels like it might be harder,

01:04:14.310 --> 01:04:16.770
then psychologically, we know that it is.

01:04:16.770 --> 01:04:19.590
When you have set yourself
up, psychologically, mentally,

01:04:19.590 --> 01:04:21.720
for that kind of failure experience,

01:04:21.720 --> 01:04:23.580
like, "I don't know that
I have the resources

01:04:23.580 --> 01:04:26.670
to get this job, this looks really hard,"

01:04:26.670 --> 01:04:29.100
you're already motivationally in a place

01:04:29.100 --> 01:04:32.820
for this task to be closer
to impossible for you.

01:04:32.820 --> 01:04:33.930
So to put it all together then,

01:04:33.930 --> 01:04:35.760
what we know is that people whose bodies

01:04:35.760 --> 01:04:38.970
might make it more challenging
for them to exercise

01:04:38.970 --> 01:04:41.310
are seeing the world in
a more challenging way

01:04:41.310 --> 01:04:43.320
and that is having these
downstream motivational

01:04:43.320 --> 01:04:44.970
and psychological effects

01:04:44.970 --> 01:04:46.920
that makes it less likely for them

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:48.870
to try to take on the
task in the first place

01:04:48.870 --> 01:04:51.210
or to experience it as harder

01:04:51.210 --> 01:04:53.303
than other people would or do.

01:04:54.810 --> 01:04:57.690
- Is the solution the same, however?

01:04:57.690 --> 01:04:59.820
Meaning, if these people are taught

01:04:59.820 --> 01:05:02.400
to adjust their visual goal line

01:05:02.400 --> 01:05:05.580
or to set a visual spotlight
on an intermediate goal,

01:05:05.580 --> 01:05:09.930
can they overcome some of
this challenge that they face

01:05:09.930 --> 01:05:12.630
simply by virtue of
their skewed perception?

01:05:12.630 --> 01:05:16.050
- Yes, so in all of the
studies that we have done,

01:05:16.050 --> 01:05:17.130
looking at that connection

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:18.960
between this narrowed focus of attention

01:05:18.960 --> 01:05:21.060
and improvements in exercise,

01:05:21.060 --> 01:05:23.190
we do not find that it only works

01:05:23.190 --> 01:05:24.300
for the people who are in shape

01:05:24.300 --> 01:05:26.640
or that it backfires for
people who are out of shape,

01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:28.350
it works for everybody.

01:05:28.350 --> 01:05:30.330
This is a strategy that
everybody can adopt,

01:05:30.330 --> 01:05:31.927
because it's just simply about like,

01:05:31.927 --> 01:05:34.200
"What do you allocate
attentional resources to?

01:05:34.200 --> 01:05:37.260
What do you sort of ignore,
and what do you focus on?"

01:05:37.260 --> 01:05:39.360
And that visually induces

01:05:39.360 --> 01:05:41.250
the same kind of illusion for everybody,

01:05:41.250 --> 01:05:43.380
regardless of whether you're overweight,

01:05:43.380 --> 01:05:44.980
or you're at your target weight,

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:47.010
or if you're struggling to get there,

01:05:47.010 --> 01:05:49.320
or you've already accomplished
where you want to be,

01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:51.690
that visual illusion can
be induced for everybody

01:05:51.690 --> 01:05:54.360
and it has the same kinds of consequences.

01:05:54.360 --> 01:05:57.900
- Terrific, earlier I made a
joke about double espresso,

01:05:57.900 --> 01:06:00.420
but now I'll make a serious
statement about double espresso,

01:06:00.420 --> 01:06:02.400
which is that it contains caffeine

01:06:02.400 --> 01:06:04.200
and caffeine as a stimulant,

01:06:04.200 --> 01:06:05.883
like all other stimulants,

01:06:07.050 --> 01:06:09.450
cause a change in our visual world.

01:06:09.450 --> 01:06:10.500
The most salient one

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:12.270
is the one that police officers look for,

01:06:12.270 --> 01:06:14.250
or parents suspecting that their kids

01:06:14.250 --> 01:06:17.970
have ingested substances
of any kind look for,

01:06:17.970 --> 01:06:20.730
which is if somebody's
pupils are unusually large

01:06:20.730 --> 01:06:23.280
for a given visual environment,

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:27.510
that is an indication of high
levels of autonomic arousal.

01:06:27.510 --> 01:06:29.310
In the street drug translation of this,

01:06:29.310 --> 01:06:31.350
people who take amphetamine or cocaine

01:06:31.350 --> 01:06:32.430
will have very big pupils.

01:06:32.430 --> 01:06:33.930
People who are very
relaxed have small pupils.

01:06:33.930 --> 01:06:35.970
However, everyone should
know that pupil size

01:06:35.970 --> 01:06:37.350
also is dynamically regulated

01:06:37.350 --> 01:06:38.820
by how bright a visual environment,

01:06:38.820 --> 01:06:41.160
so there are multiple things
controlling pupil size.

01:06:41.160 --> 01:06:44.250
However, we know that
when we are very stressed

01:06:44.250 --> 01:06:47.400
or very aroused in any
way, positive or negative,

01:06:47.400 --> 01:06:48.870
the pupils get big,

01:06:48.870 --> 01:06:50.280
but within the visual system,

01:06:50.280 --> 01:06:54.093
what that equates to is a
narrowing of the visual aperture.

01:06:55.050 --> 01:06:57.150
So rather than ingesting sugar,

01:06:57.150 --> 01:06:59.160
which I'm guessing most of the world,

01:06:59.160 --> 01:07:00.930
certainly the US needs
to ingest less sugar,

01:07:00.930 --> 01:07:02.010
at least that's what we're hearing.

01:07:02.010 --> 01:07:05.520
I'm sure there are a few
sugar sucro-nistas out there,

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:07.110
sucrose-anistas.
[Emily laughing]

01:07:07.110 --> 01:07:08.730
Who will also come after
me with pitchforks,

01:07:08.730 --> 01:07:10.680
but let's face it, most people
will probably be better off

01:07:10.680 --> 01:07:12.213
ingesting less simple sugar,

01:07:13.290 --> 01:07:15.210
but caffeine is a great motivator

01:07:15.210 --> 01:07:17.040
because of the internal sense of arousal,

01:07:17.040 --> 01:07:18.900
but it also narrows our visual window.

01:07:18.900 --> 01:07:22.320
I could imagine using
healthy amounts of caffeine

01:07:22.320 --> 01:07:25.110
combined with maybe even blinders

01:07:25.110 --> 01:07:26.790
of the sort that horses wear,

01:07:26.790 --> 01:07:28.800
maybe like a hoodie and a hat.
[Emily laughing]

01:07:28.800 --> 01:07:31.830
Maybe even blinders in order to get over

01:07:31.830 --> 01:07:33.810
some of those more challenging milestones.

01:07:33.810 --> 01:07:37.020
Is there any evidence that
people are doing this without,

01:07:37.020 --> 01:07:38.250
well, obviously people are doing it

01:07:38.250 --> 01:07:39.540
without knowledge of how it works,

01:07:39.540 --> 01:07:43.200
but are there any studies
looking at how adrenaline,

01:07:43.200 --> 01:07:46.683
or epinephrine, or any other
stimulants impact motivation?

01:07:48.538 --> 01:07:50.094
- I don't know, honestly, yeah.

01:07:50.094 --> 01:07:51.994
- [Andrew] And energy
drinks are a big thing now.

01:07:51.994 --> 01:07:54.480
- Yeah, yeah, for sure, they are,

01:07:54.480 --> 01:07:59.160
and if you actually are more
physiologically aroused,

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:02.040
or jazzed, or whatever, amped up,

01:08:02.040 --> 01:08:05.100
or you just think you are, in our studies,

01:08:05.100 --> 01:08:06.780
we have found that they
work in the same way,

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:09.420
that it can produce the
same kinds of consequences.

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:11.220
And I like that because it tells us

01:08:11.220 --> 01:08:13.170
you can actually change
the state of your body

01:08:13.170 --> 01:08:15.840
to induce these kinds of experiences,

01:08:15.840 --> 01:08:18.630
or you can just think that, [laughing]

01:08:18.630 --> 01:08:19.590
you can trick yourself,

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:21.870
you can placebo effect yourself out

01:08:21.870 --> 01:08:23.730
and produce the same kinds of effects.

01:08:23.730 --> 01:08:25.350
I had to give up coffee like 12 years ago,

01:08:25.350 --> 01:08:27.480
and not for any.
- I'm so sorry.

01:08:27.480 --> 01:08:30.990
- I love the taste, [laughing]
and so decaf is my jam,

01:08:30.990 --> 01:08:32.190
but I can't drink the caffeine,

01:08:32.190 --> 01:08:33.960
because it didn't actually do the thing

01:08:33.960 --> 01:08:35.670
that it does for so many other people,

01:08:35.670 --> 01:08:37.380
like make me feel more
energized and more awake,

01:08:37.380 --> 01:08:38.490
I just got sweaty and jittery

01:08:38.490 --> 01:08:39.780
and anxious and I couldn't focus.

01:08:39.780 --> 01:08:40.680
- Yeah, some people

01:08:40.680 --> 01:08:43.620
who already have a fairly
high baseline level

01:08:43.620 --> 01:08:45.420
of attention and motivation,

01:08:45.420 --> 01:08:47.460
they find that it puts
the autonomic seesaw

01:08:47.460 --> 01:08:49.360
too far in the sympathetic tone.

01:08:49.360 --> 01:08:50.193
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:08:50.193 --> 01:08:51.690
- And I happened to marry
the same kind of person,

01:08:51.690 --> 01:08:54.870
he also can't drink caffeine,
but loves the taste of coffee.

01:08:54.870 --> 01:08:57.240
The interesting thing is that
we both have to have coffee

01:08:57.240 --> 01:09:00.270
in the morning to feel like
we're ready to go for the day.

01:09:00.270 --> 01:09:02.760
So it's just part of our
routine [laughing] or whatever,

01:09:02.760 --> 01:09:04.860
to have that taste and have that sensation

01:09:04.860 --> 01:09:06.360
to feel like I'm ready to take on the day,

01:09:06.360 --> 01:09:07.350
even though, I mean, yeah,

01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:09.960
decaf still has some caffeine in it,

01:09:09.960 --> 01:09:11.100
but we're not drinking that much of it

01:09:11.100 --> 01:09:13.110
to probably actually create

01:09:13.110 --> 01:09:15.210
a caffeinated experience in our body,

01:09:15.210 --> 01:09:17.700
but we're tricking
ourselves psychologically

01:09:17.700 --> 01:09:19.380
into doing that thing that,

01:09:19.380 --> 01:09:22.230
in years past, used to work for us both.

01:09:22.230 --> 01:09:23.850
So I think that's
something to keep in mind,

01:09:23.850 --> 01:09:26.280
like you might have a
hoodie that you can wear

01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:28.530
to induce that visual illusion,

01:09:28.530 --> 01:09:30.390
or you can take advantage
of the power of your mind.

01:09:30.390 --> 01:09:31.950
At the end of the day, I'm a psychologist,

01:09:31.950 --> 01:09:35.430
and I believe that we
have some non-zero power

01:09:35.430 --> 01:09:37.830
over what our mind is doing,
what we're thinking about,

01:09:37.830 --> 01:09:41.310
what we allocate our attention to,

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:43.770
that can do the same kind of
thing that a hoodie might do

01:09:43.770 --> 01:09:45.480
or that a cup of caffeine might do.

01:09:45.480 --> 01:09:47.340
- Mm-hmm, I completely agree,

01:09:47.340 --> 01:09:49.800
the visual aperture is
under our conscious control.

01:09:49.800 --> 01:09:51.690
That's an amazing feature
of our visual system,

01:09:51.690 --> 01:09:53.070
we can narrow or expand it.

01:09:53.070 --> 01:09:53.903
Takes a little bit of practice, I think,

01:09:53.903 --> 01:09:55.770
for people to learn how to do this

01:09:55.770 --> 01:09:57.120
without moving their head around

01:09:57.120 --> 01:10:00.090
to expand their visual
aperture and how to narrow it,

01:10:00.090 --> 01:10:01.747
but what I always tell people is,

01:10:01.747 --> 01:10:03.810
"Just imagine a really
troubling text message

01:10:03.810 --> 01:10:05.147
or a really exciting
text message coming in,

01:10:05.147 --> 01:10:07.620
all of a sudden, you forget
about the world around you."

01:10:07.620 --> 01:10:10.290
So it can be triggered
by these outside events

01:10:10.290 --> 01:10:13.440
and we can learn how to
anchor our visual attention.

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:16.890
I'd love to ask about
other kinds of goals,

01:10:16.890 --> 01:10:18.390
meaning non-physical goals,

01:10:18.390 --> 01:10:23.390
because many people are trying
to read more, I would hope,

01:10:23.430 --> 01:10:25.560
or learn music, or a language,

01:10:25.560 --> 01:10:28.950
or things that really
involve cognitive goal lines

01:10:28.950 --> 01:10:32.400
or internal goal lines.

01:10:32.400 --> 01:10:35.010
Reading one chapter out
of a book each night

01:10:35.010 --> 01:10:37.050
is a tangible goal,

01:10:37.050 --> 01:10:40.410
the other that I've often
wondered about are these systems

01:10:40.410 --> 01:10:42.930
that allow you to
highlight individual lines

01:10:42.930 --> 01:10:44.670
or even words on a page,

01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:46.290
that's very visual obviously,

01:10:46.290 --> 01:10:48.270
and everything else is
ruled out except that word,

01:10:48.270 --> 01:10:49.650
I've always wished for books

01:10:49.650 --> 01:10:51.690
that would naturally highlight each page.

01:10:51.690 --> 01:10:53.310
And as I say that, someone
will put in the comments,

01:10:53.310 --> 01:10:54.781
this has probably existed for 10 years.

01:10:54.781 --> 01:10:56.010
[Emily laughing]
And I'm just showing

01:10:56.010 --> 01:10:57.090
what Luddite I am,

01:10:57.090 --> 01:11:02.090
but is there any example or
tactic that people could use

01:11:03.840 --> 01:11:06.510
to better approach cognitive goals,

01:11:06.510 --> 01:11:09.960
of school, work, recreational too,

01:11:09.960 --> 01:11:11.310
but that don't exist

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:14.190
in the kind of fitness and sports domain?

01:11:14.190 --> 01:11:17.130
- Totally, yeah, so just to
shout out to my brother-in-law,

01:11:17.130 --> 01:11:19.350
who has done some of that research,

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:20.430
where it does highlight

01:11:20.430 --> 01:11:22.260
different parts of words in paragraphs,

01:11:22.260 --> 01:11:23.610
and he's found it to be an effective way

01:11:23.610 --> 01:11:26.850
for English-as-a-second-language
learners to pick it up,

01:11:26.850 --> 01:11:28.500
that that is, that tying that vision

01:11:28.500 --> 01:11:30.450
to the process of learning
language is effective.

01:11:30.450 --> 01:11:34.200
And so there's a whole cool body of work

01:11:34.200 --> 01:11:35.670
and researchers looking at that,

01:11:35.670 --> 01:11:36.960
so you're right about that.

01:11:36.960 --> 01:11:39.505
- If you want to mention what he does,

01:11:39.505 --> 01:11:41.340
is there a place that people
can learn more about that?

01:11:41.340 --> 01:11:42.990
We can provide links if.

01:11:42.990 --> 01:11:43.898
- Yeah, let me.

01:11:43.898 --> 01:11:45.330
Yeah, yeah. [laughing]
- Okay, we will provide links

01:11:45.330 --> 01:11:46.350
to those resources,

01:11:46.350 --> 01:11:47.532
'cause I want those resources.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:11:47.532 --> 01:11:48.810
- I've been trying to
learn a second language

01:11:48.810 --> 01:11:50.160
for a long time.
- Yeah, cool.

01:11:50.160 --> 01:11:51.810
- I speak Spanish pretty weakly,

01:11:51.810 --> 01:11:53.572
but I would love to get better at it.

01:11:53.572 --> 01:11:54.450
- Oh, yeah.
- Okay, I'll approach you

01:11:54.450 --> 01:11:55.650
later about that.
- My five-year-old son

01:11:55.650 --> 01:11:57.524
speaks Spanish better
than I do at this point,

01:11:57.524 --> 01:11:58.357
so yeah. [laughing]
- And clearly,

01:11:58.357 --> 01:11:59.190
better than I do too,

01:11:59.190 --> 01:12:01.620
thank you. [laughing]
- Yeah, yeah,

01:12:01.620 --> 01:12:04.080
yeah, so I was thinking that too,

01:12:04.080 --> 01:12:06.300
we started this work within
the context of exercise,

01:12:06.300 --> 01:12:08.010
but of course, that's
not people's only goal

01:12:08.010 --> 01:12:09.423
that they have in life,

01:12:10.620 --> 01:12:12.780
and it isn't mine either,

01:12:12.780 --> 01:12:16.533
I have interests outside of
improving my exercise game.

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:21.268
A couple years ago, when
I was writing the book,

01:12:21.268 --> 01:12:22.410
I also had a child,

01:12:22.410 --> 01:12:24.660
the same month that I had the opportunity

01:12:24.660 --> 01:12:26.850
to pull all this research together

01:12:26.850 --> 01:12:29.703
is the same month that my son came to be.

01:12:31.713 --> 01:12:32.730
And I started to realize

01:12:32.730 --> 01:12:35.820
I became a lot less
interesting once he was around,

01:12:35.820 --> 01:12:37.110
he was fascinating,

01:12:37.110 --> 01:12:39.357
but I was changing diapers and feeding him

01:12:39.357 --> 01:12:40.200
and that was it.

01:12:40.200 --> 01:12:41.910
People would come over,
be like, "What's up?

01:12:41.910 --> 01:12:42.743
How have you been?

01:12:42.743 --> 01:12:44.097
Tell me something that's
going on in your life,"

01:12:44.097 --> 01:12:47.160
and all I had to talk
about was what was boring?

01:12:47.160 --> 01:12:48.810
And I just felt like, "I've lost myself,"

01:12:48.810 --> 01:12:51.180
I used to pride myself on crazy adventures

01:12:51.180 --> 01:12:52.590
and problems I would get myself in

01:12:52.590 --> 01:12:54.300
and I was a great storyteller,

01:12:54.300 --> 01:12:55.710
and that, all of a sudden, disappeared

01:12:55.710 --> 01:12:56.970
as soon as he came into the world,

01:12:56.970 --> 01:12:59.280
because he became my world.

01:12:59.280 --> 01:13:00.157
So then I started thinking, like,

01:13:00.157 --> 01:13:02.610
"I need to pull back some coolness,

01:13:02.610 --> 01:13:03.960
if I ever had it in the first place,

01:13:03.960 --> 01:13:05.400
but I need to be a cooler person

01:13:05.400 --> 01:13:07.140
than I'm coming across right now,"

01:13:07.140 --> 01:13:09.947
so I decided, "I want
to learn to play drums,

01:13:09.947 --> 01:13:14.520
and I want to be a one-hit
wonder as a rockstar drummer.

01:13:14.520 --> 01:13:15.480
I only want one song,

01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:17.160
'cause I know I'm not going to
be able to do more than that."

01:13:17.160 --> 01:13:19.650
I'm not coordinated at all,

01:13:19.650 --> 01:13:22.110
from the beginning of time,

01:13:22.110 --> 01:13:25.020
in fifth grade, I have this
really vivid flash bulb memory

01:13:25.020 --> 01:13:27.000
of playing basketball
for the very first time,

01:13:27.000 --> 01:13:29.220
I lost my footing, I knocked
into my own teammate,

01:13:29.220 --> 01:13:30.780
pushed her out of bounds
while she had the ball,

01:13:30.780 --> 01:13:32.694
we lost the game and
I was not invited back

01:13:32.694 --> 01:13:34.920
[laughing] on the team
for the next season.

01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:39.840
And so that fomented my
self-definition of uncoordinated.

01:13:39.840 --> 01:13:42.360
I am a musician, but I am not a drummer,

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:45.870
and the idea of coordinating
four limbs in real time

01:13:45.870 --> 01:13:49.320
was like, "If I could do
that, I would be so proud."

01:13:49.320 --> 01:13:51.120
So that's a goal that I set for myself,

01:13:51.120 --> 01:13:53.610
at the same time that my
son came into this world,

01:13:53.610 --> 01:13:56.730
when I was also trying to
think about goal setting

01:13:56.730 --> 01:13:58.740
and how to improve my ability

01:13:58.740 --> 01:14:01.260
and all of our ability to get a job done

01:14:01.260 --> 01:14:04.500
when you're faced with
some pretty big obstacles.

01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:06.270
So I got to practice all these techniques

01:14:06.270 --> 01:14:08.700
that we're talking about on
myself and see for myself,

01:14:08.700 --> 01:14:10.500
when I tell people, "Hey, try this thing,

01:14:10.500 --> 01:14:11.460
narrowed focus of attention,

01:14:11.460 --> 01:14:15.210
does it help with something
like becoming a better drummer?"

01:14:15.210 --> 01:14:16.320
And the answer is yeah,

01:14:16.320 --> 01:14:17.940
these tactics at least work for me,

01:14:17.940 --> 01:14:19.590
sometimes, under some circumstances,

01:14:19.590 --> 01:14:20.850
and they do for other people who try them

01:14:20.850 --> 01:14:23.763
for other goals that aren't
necessarily about exercise.

01:14:25.170 --> 01:14:28.060
One that I found particularly helpful

01:14:29.160 --> 01:14:31.410
was overcoming my bad memory,

01:14:31.410 --> 01:14:34.440
that everybody's memories
are faulty, right?

01:14:34.440 --> 01:14:37.800
Everybody has sort of a
warped perception of the past,

01:14:37.800 --> 01:14:40.440
it might be skewed more
positively than maybe we deserve,

01:14:40.440 --> 01:14:42.300
or it might be skewed more negatively

01:14:42.300 --> 01:14:45.510
if you feel that what
looms large in your mind,

01:14:45.510 --> 01:14:47.490
as you reflect on something from the past,

01:14:47.490 --> 01:14:49.020
or the mistakes that you've made,

01:14:49.020 --> 01:14:52.320
or the social faux pas that you had,

01:14:52.320 --> 01:14:54.960
or challenges that you faced at work

01:14:54.960 --> 01:14:57.360
when you got in trouble with
a boss or with a colleague,

01:14:57.360 --> 01:15:00.390
if that's what really
stands out in your mind,

01:15:00.390 --> 01:15:02.970
or the good side of all
of those possibilities,

01:15:02.970 --> 01:15:04.950
we probably aren't
getting the world right.

01:15:04.950 --> 01:15:08.100
And that is something that
our brain has evolved,

01:15:08.100 --> 01:15:09.660
to give us a faulty memory,

01:15:09.660 --> 01:15:12.690
to level and sharpen, to
not encode and remember

01:15:12.690 --> 01:15:15.060
and be able to recall everything
that we've experienced

01:15:15.060 --> 01:15:16.683
with accuracy and precision.

01:15:17.520 --> 01:15:18.660
And that's a problem

01:15:18.660 --> 01:15:22.140
when it comes to assessing
our own goal progress.

01:15:22.140 --> 01:15:24.360
When we want to be our own accountant

01:15:24.360 --> 01:15:26.730
and try to determine, "How are we doing?

01:15:26.730 --> 01:15:28.590
If I want to become a drummer,

01:15:28.590 --> 01:15:31.950
am I on track for getting there before X,

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:33.573
before my time runs out?

01:15:34.500 --> 01:15:36.030
Am I going to make it or not?"

01:15:36.030 --> 01:15:37.260
And I think that's an experience,

01:15:37.260 --> 01:15:38.280
whether they want to be a drummer or not,

01:15:38.280 --> 01:15:39.660
that a lot of people can resonate with,

01:15:39.660 --> 01:15:42.660
of trying to determine,
"Is this trajectory,

01:15:42.660 --> 01:15:45.030
is this rate of progress
going to get the job done

01:15:45.030 --> 01:15:46.260
by X amount of time?

01:15:46.260 --> 01:15:48.210
Will I have my swimsuit body by summer?"

01:15:48.210 --> 01:15:50.580
Or, "Will I save enough for retirement

01:15:50.580 --> 01:15:52.740
by the time I hit 65?"

01:15:52.740 --> 01:15:54.690
For these goals where time is involved

01:15:54.690 --> 01:15:56.370
and there is a deadline,

01:15:56.370 --> 01:16:01.080
we do take moments to
assess our trajectory.

01:16:01.080 --> 01:16:03.450
And if we just rely on our memory,

01:16:03.450 --> 01:16:05.460
we're probably going to do a bad job

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:08.760
of assessing that trajectory,

01:16:08.760 --> 01:16:10.680
of knowing whether we're on pace

01:16:10.680 --> 01:16:12.330
to meeting our deadline.

01:16:12.330 --> 01:16:14.050
And I found that to be the
case as I was thinking about,

01:16:14.050 --> 01:16:16.290
"Am I actually going to be
able to learn this song?

01:16:16.290 --> 01:16:17.970
I mean, I know that
it's going a lot slower

01:16:17.970 --> 01:16:20.130
than it probably would for anybody else,

01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:22.500
but to give myself a
deadline and a commitment."

01:16:22.500 --> 01:16:23.970
I decided I was going to put on a show,

01:16:23.970 --> 01:16:25.830
I was going to invite everybody I knew

01:16:25.830 --> 01:16:27.390
and also people I didn't know,

01:16:27.390 --> 01:16:31.140
and I was going to play
my one song for them, so.

01:16:31.140 --> 01:16:32.357
- This was while writing a book

01:16:32.357 --> 01:16:33.600
and having just had a child.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:16:33.600 --> 01:16:35.310
- Yeah.
- So when you read the book,

01:16:35.310 --> 01:16:37.350
you'll see my story,

01:16:37.350 --> 01:16:39.303
and it's the real truth of it.

01:16:40.708 --> 01:16:43.890
I mean, I did play that
show and it was fine.

01:16:43.890 --> 01:16:46.110
And then, because I wrote
about it in the book,

01:16:46.110 --> 01:16:48.030
then some other opportunities

01:16:48.030 --> 01:16:49.980
to play it publicly have come up.

01:16:49.980 --> 01:16:52.410
And it's like, "All right, I
told people I can play drums,

01:16:52.410 --> 01:16:53.460
I better show them

01:16:53.460 --> 01:16:55.200
that I actually still can play this song."

01:16:55.200 --> 01:16:56.220
- [Andrew] I love it.

01:16:56.220 --> 01:16:57.750
- Yeah, so that that's been fun.

01:16:57.750 --> 01:16:59.190
I have become a one-hit wonder,

01:16:59.190 --> 01:17:01.980
if you ask me to play
the song, like, "Encore,"

01:17:01.980 --> 01:17:03.930
it's just going to get that
same song a second time,

01:17:03.930 --> 01:17:05.910
so literally one-hit wonder.

01:17:05.910 --> 01:17:07.927
But so in the process of figuring out,

01:17:07.927 --> 01:17:09.540
"Am I going to be able to play this show?

01:17:09.540 --> 01:17:11.970
I sent out invitations,
the date is committed,

01:17:11.970 --> 01:17:14.910
people are coming to
listen to my one song,

01:17:14.910 --> 01:17:18.567
God bless them, how's it going to go?"

01:17:19.597 --> 01:17:20.797
And it felt awful, it just felt like,

01:17:20.797 --> 01:17:22.740
"I am not making progress here,"

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:24.330
because there's a lot more things

01:17:24.330 --> 01:17:25.710
that actually are pressing, right?

01:17:25.710 --> 01:17:28.050
Like the kid does need to get fed,

01:17:28.050 --> 01:17:29.850
I do have to go to my day job,

01:17:29.850 --> 01:17:33.030
the editor is asking for
the next draft of this book,

01:17:33.030 --> 01:17:34.590
and that is going to take precedence,

01:17:34.590 --> 01:17:35.850
like it does for so many people,

01:17:35.850 --> 01:17:38.850
that things command your bandwidth,

01:17:38.850 --> 01:17:40.767
even when you have this goal
that you've committed to

01:17:40.767 --> 01:17:43.410
and that you've got on the books.

01:17:43.410 --> 01:17:45.390
And so I just felt this looming anxiety

01:17:45.390 --> 01:17:48.300
about this goal that would require,

01:17:48.300 --> 01:17:49.650
didn't have to be daily practice,

01:17:49.650 --> 01:17:53.430
but you can't cram that kind of a goal,

01:17:53.430 --> 01:17:56.280
it does take committed investment

01:17:56.280 --> 01:17:58.527
for a sustained period of time.

01:17:58.527 --> 01:18:00.270
And so I had this looming anxiety

01:18:00.270 --> 01:18:02.670
that I'm not making good enough progress,

01:18:02.670 --> 01:18:04.440
but that's because I
was relying on my memory

01:18:04.440 --> 01:18:06.247
and my brain to recall, like,

01:18:06.247 --> 01:18:07.470
"How many times did you practice?

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:08.880
What was it like the
last time you practiced?

01:18:08.880 --> 01:18:11.460
What was it like when you
tried to play this bit

01:18:11.460 --> 01:18:13.230
or this riff like two weeks ago,

01:18:13.230 --> 01:18:15.210
have you gotten any better since then?"

01:18:15.210 --> 01:18:18.060
And it just felt like, "No,
I haven't practiced enough.

01:18:18.060 --> 01:18:19.650
I don't remember when the
last time I played was,

01:18:19.650 --> 01:18:22.170
but it definitely doesn't feel
like I'm getting any better."

01:18:22.170 --> 01:18:23.610
Then I thought, "You know what?

01:18:23.610 --> 01:18:26.310
I should stop relying
on my brain to tell me,

01:18:26.310 --> 01:18:30.390
where am I at and am I
on an upward slope here?

01:18:30.390 --> 01:18:31.410
I need to look at the data."

01:18:31.410 --> 01:18:33.270
I love data, scientists love data,

01:18:33.270 --> 01:18:35.640
so I started to collect data on myself.

01:18:35.640 --> 01:18:37.860
What I did was download this app

01:18:37.860 --> 01:18:40.380
that a friend had told me
about called the Reporter App,

01:18:40.380 --> 01:18:42.450
there's lots of these
kinds of things out there.

01:18:42.450 --> 01:18:44.400
Basically, it just sets up your phone

01:18:44.400 --> 01:18:45.840
to randomly ping you

01:18:45.840 --> 01:18:48.300
with whatever questions
you want your phone to ask.

01:18:48.300 --> 01:18:50.370
It records your answers,
you can download the data,

01:18:50.370 --> 01:18:54.540
you can make pretty graphs
to see, "What's my change

01:18:54.540 --> 01:18:57.840
and how I've answered
these questions over time?"

01:18:57.840 --> 01:18:59.130
So I did that for a month,

01:18:59.130 --> 01:19:03.450
for a month, I had my phone
ask me a couple of times a day,

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:05.400
well, maybe twice a day
really, "Did you practice,

01:19:05.400 --> 01:19:07.987
since last time I asked
you," my phone says,

01:19:07.987 --> 01:19:09.600
"did you practice?"

01:19:09.600 --> 01:19:10.740
Mostly it was no,

01:19:10.740 --> 01:19:12.540
and if yes, then it would funnel

01:19:12.540 --> 01:19:13.620
a couple of other questions,

01:19:13.620 --> 01:19:15.900
like, "How did you do, how do you feel?

01:19:15.900 --> 01:19:17.790
Check a couple of
different emotion words now

01:19:17.790 --> 01:19:20.007
about your experience when you played."

01:19:21.480 --> 01:19:22.650
And I did that for a month,

01:19:22.650 --> 01:19:25.500
after a month, went into my
office, downloaded the data,

01:19:25.500 --> 01:19:28.230
and first took stock before
I looked at the numbers,

01:19:28.230 --> 01:19:30.810
like, "How do I think I
did over the last month?"

01:19:30.810 --> 01:19:33.840
And I thought, "Same as every other month,

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:34.890
I didn't really get anywhere.

01:19:34.890 --> 01:19:37.290
Yeah, I practiced, but
I still feel awful."

01:19:37.290 --> 01:19:39.600
And I cried, I cried having to practice.

01:19:39.600 --> 01:19:41.940
I was upset with myself
for setting this goal

01:19:41.940 --> 01:19:43.590
and feeling so anxious about it.

01:19:43.590 --> 01:19:45.000
All I remember is that I cried,

01:19:45.000 --> 01:19:47.790
cried too much about
this personal conquest

01:19:47.790 --> 01:19:49.200
that wouldn't matter to anybody else.

01:19:49.200 --> 01:19:50.220
Honestly, it really doesn't matter

01:19:50.220 --> 01:19:51.570
in the scope of things anyway,

01:19:51.570 --> 01:19:54.150
I'm not going to become
a drummer professionally,

01:19:54.150 --> 01:19:57.720
so who cares if I
embarrass myself publicly?

01:19:57.720 --> 01:19:59.490
But what I found from the data

01:19:59.490 --> 01:20:00.810
was my memory was totally wrong,

01:20:00.810 --> 01:20:04.710
I actually had practiced far
more times than I remembered,

01:20:04.710 --> 01:20:07.890
and when I looked at my
emotion words that I used,

01:20:07.890 --> 01:20:09.900
it was a clear upward trajectory.

01:20:09.900 --> 01:20:11.250
Yeah, I did cry,

01:20:11.250 --> 01:20:13.830
that part I hadn't
misremembered or made up,

01:20:13.830 --> 01:20:15.090
but by the end of that month,

01:20:15.090 --> 01:20:16.800
I had gotten a compliment from my husband,

01:20:16.800 --> 01:20:18.210
who actually is a drummer,

01:20:18.210 --> 01:20:20.310
and said like, "Hey,
that wasn't that bad."

01:20:20.310 --> 01:20:21.457
And then there was one expletive,

01:20:21.457 --> 01:20:23.280
"You were effing amazing [laughing]

01:20:23.280 --> 01:20:25.620
at that one thing you'd
been practicing at."

01:20:25.620 --> 01:20:28.320
But like, "Okay, fine, he's my
husband, right, is he just?"

01:20:28.320 --> 01:20:30.720
So at the moment, it didn't
really feel that great,

01:20:30.720 --> 01:20:32.010
and I downplayed it, and as a result,

01:20:32.010 --> 01:20:33.810
it didn't stick in my brain, right?

01:20:33.810 --> 01:20:35.880
I remember how stupid it felt that I cried

01:20:35.880 --> 01:20:38.670
because I can't make progress,

01:20:38.670 --> 01:20:39.990
and I downplayed in my mind

01:20:39.990 --> 01:20:41.040
the thing that actually should've been

01:20:41.040 --> 01:20:44.370
a legitimate indicator that
progress was being made.

01:20:44.370 --> 01:20:46.590
So all of which is to say,

01:20:46.590 --> 01:20:49.800
I needed to collect that data on myself

01:20:49.800 --> 01:20:53.430
and to look at it objectively,
accurately and completely,

01:20:53.430 --> 01:20:56.550
because my brain wasn't doing that for me.

01:20:56.550 --> 01:21:01.350
That visual experience [laughing]
of downloading that data

01:21:01.350 --> 01:21:04.720
and looking at what was
my actual experience

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:06.750
gave me better insight

01:21:06.750 --> 01:21:10.590
as I was trying to assess the
trajectory of my progress,

01:21:10.590 --> 01:21:14.280
I became a more accurate
accountant of my own progress,

01:21:14.280 --> 01:21:16.590
which is important for setting goals

01:21:16.590 --> 01:21:18.780
or resetting them when
you need to calibrate

01:21:18.780 --> 01:21:20.460
in light of what's left to do

01:21:20.460 --> 01:21:22.423
and how much time do you have to do it in?

01:21:22.423 --> 01:21:26.163
- I love it, so basically,
if I understand correctly,

01:21:27.360 --> 01:21:29.100
when the intermediate goals

01:21:29.100 --> 01:21:32.040
of say daily practice,
or twice-a-day practice,

01:21:32.040 --> 01:21:34.170
or reading, or math, et cetera,

01:21:34.170 --> 01:21:36.690
are not a visual goal line,

01:21:36.690 --> 01:21:38.880
it really does help to visualize

01:21:38.880 --> 01:21:41.580
some aspect related to
that non-visual goal line,

01:21:41.580 --> 01:21:44.610
in this case, the Reporter
App was a useful tool.

01:21:44.610 --> 01:21:46.680
I've never heard of it, I plan to use it,

01:21:46.680 --> 01:21:48.480
I'm sure a number of people
will be interested in it,

01:21:48.480 --> 01:21:49.650
sounds like there are others out there,

01:21:49.650 --> 01:21:51.480
but that's the one that
you found most useful?

01:21:51.480 --> 01:21:53.760
- Yeah, yeah, there's another one too

01:21:53.760 --> 01:21:56.366
that is even more visual than that,

01:21:56.366 --> 01:21:57.300
[laughing] than the Reporter App,

01:21:57.300 --> 01:21:58.590
although that has visual components

01:21:58.590 --> 01:22:00.450
and is really effective if you like data

01:22:00.450 --> 01:22:03.810
and want to collect numbers on
yourself or your experience,

01:22:03.810 --> 01:22:06.780
there's another one called
the 1 Second Everyday app.

01:22:06.780 --> 01:22:10.120
This is really awesome
because the app is a mechanism

01:22:12.354 --> 01:22:15.120
to record one second of your life.

01:22:15.120 --> 01:22:17.160
There's such an awesome
community of people

01:22:17.160 --> 01:22:20.463
that just live by this and
love having these experiences,

01:22:21.990 --> 01:22:25.050
and the creator of it I got
to a chance to talk with,

01:22:25.050 --> 01:22:26.100
and he has done this,

01:22:26.100 --> 01:22:29.520
he's taken a one-second video
of some aspect of his life,

01:22:29.520 --> 01:22:32.310
every day, for, I don't know, 12 years,

01:22:32.310 --> 01:22:33.893
13 years or something.
- One second?

01:22:33.893 --> 01:22:35.760
- Yeah, one second.

01:22:35.760 --> 01:22:38.700
And then what the app
does is smash 'em together

01:22:38.700 --> 01:22:41.580
and give you a chronology
of what your year,

01:22:41.580 --> 01:22:45.060
or your month, or your last
decade of life has been like,

01:22:45.060 --> 01:22:47.280
and presents it as like a
streamlined video for you.

01:22:47.280 --> 01:22:49.140
So you just see these flashes

01:22:49.140 --> 01:22:51.660
of your life over however
long you tell the app

01:22:51.660 --> 01:22:54.690
to create a montage for you.

01:22:54.690 --> 01:22:56.400
And so when you see these
videos that people have made,

01:22:56.400 --> 01:22:57.390
especially those that have been doing it

01:22:57.390 --> 01:22:59.250
for a really long time, it's fascinating.

01:22:59.250 --> 01:23:01.110
I did that for myself too, I tried it,

01:23:01.110 --> 01:23:04.560
one second of today's drumming
performance, another second,

01:23:04.560 --> 01:23:05.707
it's not enough to capture,

01:23:05.707 --> 01:23:07.350
"Am I actually doing a
good job of drumming?"

01:23:07.350 --> 01:23:09.330
or, "What's my trajectory for drumming?"

01:23:09.330 --> 01:23:10.620
But the guy who made it says

01:23:10.620 --> 01:23:14.970
one of the most awesome one-second
videos that he ever made

01:23:14.970 --> 01:23:16.120
is of a brick wall.

01:23:16.120 --> 01:23:18.240
I was like, "Well, you
didn't need a video of that,

01:23:18.240 --> 01:23:19.470
what's the wall doing?"

01:23:19.470 --> 01:23:20.303
It's not crumbling,

01:23:20.303 --> 01:23:22.290
it's not in earthquake land
or something like that,

01:23:22.290 --> 01:23:25.867
it's just slightly jittery,
one second of a brick wall.

01:23:25.867 --> 01:23:28.500
And I was like, "How is that motivating

01:23:28.500 --> 01:23:29.820
or exciting to you, why is that?

01:23:29.820 --> 01:23:32.760
You've been doing this for 13
years every day, one second,

01:23:32.760 --> 01:23:35.340
why is that the one second
that matters to you most?"

01:23:35.340 --> 01:23:38.400
And he says, "Because when
it comes up in my montage,

01:23:38.400 --> 01:23:42.000
it reminds me of a really
horrific moment in my family.

01:23:42.000 --> 01:23:43.740
That was the first wall that I saw

01:23:43.740 --> 01:23:44.670
when I walked out of the room

01:23:44.670 --> 01:23:46.560
having heard that my sister-in-law

01:23:46.560 --> 01:23:50.460
had this awful, awful experience,

01:23:50.460 --> 01:23:52.560
her intestines started
to twist up on themselves

01:23:52.560 --> 01:23:53.393
and knot up,

01:23:53.393 --> 01:23:56.400
and she was on the brink of death.

01:23:56.400 --> 01:23:57.930
And we had just found this out,

01:23:57.930 --> 01:23:59.220
she had just gotten into the hospital,

01:23:59.220 --> 01:24:02.430
they diagnosed this issue that
required immediate surgery.

01:24:02.430 --> 01:24:04.080
And our family was there
to hear about this,

01:24:04.080 --> 01:24:07.110
and we were all stunned
that she might die,

01:24:07.110 --> 01:24:09.060
like, 'Right now, she might die.'

01:24:09.060 --> 01:24:10.320
And that's the first thing that I saw,

01:24:10.320 --> 01:24:13.260
and it reminds me of how precious life is,

01:24:13.260 --> 01:24:14.610
how important family is

01:24:14.610 --> 01:24:15.750
and how the rest of whatever

01:24:15.750 --> 01:24:17.610
we were doing that day didn't matter,

01:24:17.610 --> 01:24:19.860
because we all needed to be
here together right now."

01:24:19.860 --> 01:24:24.270
And that is all of this
emotion and purpose in life

01:24:24.270 --> 01:24:25.740
is conjured up or reminded

01:24:25.740 --> 01:24:29.010
when he looks at one
second of a brick wall

01:24:29.010 --> 01:24:30.720
as it pops into his video feed.

01:24:30.720 --> 01:24:32.580
So if you're visually oriented

01:24:32.580 --> 01:24:33.877
and you do want ways to remember,

01:24:33.877 --> 01:24:35.310
"What was life like,

01:24:35.310 --> 01:24:38.490
what has my year in review,
what does it look like?"

01:24:38.490 --> 01:24:40.860
that's an awesome app, 1 second Everyday,

01:24:40.860 --> 01:24:42.930
that can help you do that.

01:24:42.930 --> 01:24:44.370
- These are great recommendations.

01:24:44.370 --> 01:24:46.320
And a couple of reflections,

01:24:46.320 --> 01:24:49.470
first of all, the brick wall example

01:24:49.470 --> 01:24:52.530
is a beautiful way of highlighting

01:24:52.530 --> 01:24:53.910
this other feature of the visual system,

01:24:53.910 --> 01:24:56.760
which is that the brain
largely thinks in symbols,

01:24:56.760 --> 01:24:57.900
it's very efficient,

01:24:57.900 --> 01:25:00.870
it batches entire
experiences into symbols,

01:25:00.870 --> 01:25:01.890
and in this case, the brick wall

01:25:01.890 --> 01:25:03.600
can be attached to a
whole set of experiences

01:25:03.600 --> 01:25:05.910
that are very meaningful
to this individual,

01:25:05.910 --> 01:25:08.010
that brick walls don't mean that,

01:25:08.010 --> 01:25:10.440
or didn't mean that to
me until hearing this.

01:25:10.440 --> 01:25:12.300
So I think that it highlights

01:25:12.300 --> 01:25:15.060
the fact that the actual
symbol is less relevant

01:25:15.060 --> 01:25:17.040
than what we attach to that symbol,

01:25:17.040 --> 01:25:19.320
but that symbols are so efficient

01:25:19.320 --> 01:25:21.570
that even in a one-second
view of something,

01:25:21.570 --> 01:25:24.630
we can attach to it,
for better or for worse.

01:25:24.630 --> 01:25:29.100
The other is that I'm a
absolute, almost rabid proponent

01:25:29.100 --> 01:25:31.530
of people getting morning
sunlight in their eyes

01:25:31.530 --> 01:25:34.500
as the fundamental layer of
setting their circadian rhythms

01:25:34.500 --> 01:25:36.660
and sleep and health as
a zero-cost practice,

01:25:36.660 --> 01:25:37.800
that believe it or not, can be done

01:25:37.800 --> 01:25:39.025
any time of year, or anywhere.

01:25:39.025 --> 01:25:40.020
[Emily laughing]

01:25:40.020 --> 01:25:41.790
But it does take a little bit of effort,

01:25:41.790 --> 01:25:42.810
you have to get outside,

01:25:42.810 --> 01:25:43.860
you can't do it through a window

01:25:43.860 --> 01:25:45.330
or a windshield for it to be efficient,

01:25:45.330 --> 01:25:47.880
but it has huge outsize
effects on human health,

01:25:47.880 --> 01:25:51.090
this has now been demonstrated
again and again and again.

01:25:51.090 --> 01:25:54.180
And so I'm going to just do
a sort of call to action,

01:25:54.180 --> 01:25:55.260
if people aren't already doing this,

01:25:55.260 --> 01:25:57.060
I'm going to start using the 1 Second app

01:25:57.060 --> 01:25:59.580
to record my morning sunlight viewing,

01:25:59.580 --> 01:26:01.260
and prove that even through cloud cover,

01:26:01.260 --> 01:26:03.870
you're getting more photons
than you are indoors

01:26:03.870 --> 01:26:04.950
and that it's worthwhile.

01:26:04.950 --> 01:26:07.200
I also would love to do
this for my next dog,

01:26:07.200 --> 01:26:09.960
to go from puppy to full-size dog,

01:26:09.960 --> 01:26:12.003
and maybe even to the end, who knows?

01:26:13.650 --> 01:26:15.420
Great, these are wonderful tools,

01:26:15.420 --> 01:26:18.330
you've given us a huge
number of practical tools,

01:26:18.330 --> 01:26:21.600
which, frankly, isn't always
the case on these podcasts.

01:26:21.600 --> 01:26:23.730
We always strive to do science
and science-based tools

01:26:23.730 --> 01:26:24.720
as our kind of mantra,

01:26:24.720 --> 01:26:27.963
but you've given a rich
set of tools here to apply.

01:26:29.280 --> 01:26:31.740
I just want to briefly
backtrack to something,

01:26:31.740 --> 01:26:33.600
and then a final question.

01:26:33.600 --> 01:26:36.090
Earlier, we were talking
about how unfit people

01:26:36.090 --> 01:26:37.470
see the world as more challenging,

01:26:37.470 --> 01:26:40.230
maybe even hills as steeper,
distances as further,

01:26:40.230 --> 01:26:42.750
and how shifting people
into a state of energy,

01:26:42.750 --> 01:26:46.470
either cognitively or through
the ingestion of real glucose

01:26:46.470 --> 01:26:48.630
to get an energetic lift,
or maybe through caffeine

01:26:48.630 --> 01:26:49.830
if that's within their practice

01:26:49.830 --> 01:26:53.133
and span of healthy
behaviors, they could do that.

01:26:55.230 --> 01:26:57.960
There's so many people who
are suffering from depression,

01:26:57.960 --> 01:27:01.530
which one of the key
features of depression

01:27:01.530 --> 01:27:02.520
is a lack of energy,

01:27:02.520 --> 01:27:04.020
even though there can be an anxiety

01:27:04.020 --> 01:27:05.370
associated with depression,

01:27:06.540 --> 01:27:09.660
I have to wonder whether
or not some of these tools

01:27:09.660 --> 01:27:11.820
are being deployed or will be deployed

01:27:11.820 --> 01:27:13.290
in the context of mental health,

01:27:13.290 --> 01:27:16.260
because depression is
this vicious loop, right?

01:27:16.260 --> 01:27:19.740
People feel a lack of
energy and hopelessness,

01:27:19.740 --> 01:27:21.450
and then things just look harder,

01:27:21.450 --> 01:27:24.690
and so then it just verifies
their negative worldview

01:27:24.690 --> 01:27:26.130
and it's a downward spiral.

01:27:26.130 --> 01:27:28.560
That's why medication, in some cases,

01:27:28.560 --> 01:27:30.210
and social sport, et
cetera, can be helpful,

01:27:30.210 --> 01:27:31.890
because they feel more energized,

01:27:31.890 --> 01:27:34.740
the side effects, often,
are a problem, however.

01:27:34.740 --> 01:27:37.380
Have there been any efforts to implement

01:27:37.380 --> 01:27:39.600
some of these visual tools

01:27:39.600 --> 01:27:42.690
to create this increase
in systolic blood pressure

01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:44.130
and a kind of readiness and willingness

01:27:44.130 --> 01:27:46.950
to lean into what people
perceive as immense challenge?

01:27:46.950 --> 01:27:48.600
And if not, for anyone listening,

01:27:48.600 --> 01:27:50.850
I know we have a lot of listeners
in the mental health space

01:27:50.850 --> 01:27:53.790
and in the helping space, so to speak,

01:27:53.790 --> 01:27:57.930
I can imagine these are zero cost, right?

01:27:57.930 --> 01:28:00.120
We all provide, with
people that are sighted,

01:28:00.120 --> 01:28:02.880
have the apparati to do it.

01:28:02.880 --> 01:28:04.290
Are you aware of any studies like this

01:28:04.290 --> 01:28:06.060
or is your laboratory
involved in any studies?

01:28:06.060 --> 01:28:08.100
'Cause I just see an immense value

01:28:08.100 --> 01:28:11.460
of implementing the sorts of
tools that you've developed.

01:28:11.460 --> 01:28:14.820
- Yeah, we haven't explored
those ideas directly.

01:28:14.820 --> 01:28:17.130
So call to all the scientists
that are out there,

01:28:17.130 --> 01:28:19.980
there's a great opportunity to
start looking at these tools

01:28:19.980 --> 01:28:22.320
within the mental health
space, you're right.

01:28:22.320 --> 01:28:24.780
Other researchers though have,

01:28:24.780 --> 01:28:29.520
not this use of inducing a
narrowed attentional focus

01:28:29.520 --> 01:28:32.670
and can they now feel more
energized to go for a run?

01:28:32.670 --> 01:28:34.830
But they have looked at the
relationship between anxiety,

01:28:34.830 --> 01:28:37.380
depression and visual experience,

01:28:37.380 --> 01:28:40.320
and found, over decades,

01:28:40.320 --> 01:28:42.900
evidence that people with
depression or with anxiety,

01:28:42.900 --> 01:28:45.450
what their attention is captured by

01:28:45.450 --> 01:28:48.240
within the bigger global surrounding world

01:28:48.240 --> 01:28:49.740
are those things that are negative

01:28:49.740 --> 01:28:51.540
or reinforcing of their worldview.

01:28:51.540 --> 01:28:52.620
Now, that happens for everybody,

01:28:52.620 --> 01:28:55.620
that things that are on our
mind tend to pop out, [laughing]

01:28:55.620 --> 01:28:56.940
that whatever we're thinking about,

01:28:56.940 --> 01:28:59.370
we might start seeing some version of it

01:28:59.370 --> 01:29:00.750
showing up in the world around us

01:29:00.750 --> 01:29:02.670
that captures our attention.

01:29:02.670 --> 01:29:04.410
That's an idea called priming,

01:29:04.410 --> 01:29:06.730
what we're thinking
about might then lead us

01:29:07.935 --> 01:29:09.090
to attend to the world,

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:10.740
to see things in a way that aligns

01:29:10.740 --> 01:29:12.180
with what we're already thinking about,

01:29:12.180 --> 01:29:13.770
it's just that when what
we're thinking about

01:29:13.770 --> 01:29:18.770
are those depressive, ruminative,
anxiety, fearful thoughts,

01:29:18.840 --> 01:29:20.700
when that is what is
cognitively accessible,

01:29:20.700 --> 01:29:22.260
when that's what's going through our mind,

01:29:22.260 --> 01:29:24.930
then that's also what
captures our visual gaze.

01:29:24.930 --> 01:29:27.180
So when we think like, "The world is hard,

01:29:27.180 --> 01:29:29.370
the world is full of sadness,"

01:29:29.370 --> 01:29:30.720
and that's the thought in our mind,

01:29:30.720 --> 01:29:32.460
and then we start seeing the people

01:29:32.460 --> 01:29:33.750
with frowns on their faces

01:29:33.750 --> 01:29:35.880
or who are experiencing anxiety,

01:29:35.880 --> 01:29:37.560
and that's what captures our attention,

01:29:37.560 --> 01:29:39.150
even when there's other people around

01:29:39.150 --> 01:29:40.400
that might not be seeing the world

01:29:40.400 --> 01:29:42.600
or experiencing the world that way,

01:29:42.600 --> 01:29:44.130
it becomes reinforcing.

01:29:44.130 --> 01:29:46.140
When I think that the
world is threatening,

01:29:46.140 --> 01:29:48.330
and then I notice the
threats that are around me,

01:29:48.330 --> 01:29:49.410
that confirms what I'm thinking,

01:29:49.410 --> 01:29:51.690
which heightens my anxiety or my fear,

01:29:51.690 --> 01:29:53.490
and then it further leads me

01:29:53.490 --> 01:29:56.340
to narrowly focus on those
elements of the environment

01:29:56.340 --> 01:29:58.290
that are aligned with that worldview,

01:29:58.290 --> 01:30:00.120
it's really hard to get out of that,

01:30:00.120 --> 01:30:02.670
that's where the vicious
cycle can come from.

01:30:02.670 --> 01:30:05.340
So that has been really well established

01:30:05.340 --> 01:30:09.390
within the medical community,
this selective attention

01:30:09.390 --> 01:30:13.593
relating to states of mental unwellness,

01:30:14.610 --> 01:30:16.410
that's been pretty well established.

01:30:17.855 --> 01:30:19.740
And so there's been
some interventions done

01:30:19.740 --> 01:30:22.320
with people that have
depression or anxiety,

01:30:22.320 --> 01:30:24.570
saying like, "Here's an array,

01:30:24.570 --> 01:30:26.520
a photograph of a bunch
of different faces.

01:30:26.520 --> 01:30:27.353
Yes, it's artificial,

01:30:27.353 --> 01:30:28.950
it kind of looks like
a page from a yearbook,

01:30:28.950 --> 01:30:30.420
a high school yearbook,

01:30:30.420 --> 01:30:32.310
but look for the faces that are smiling,

01:30:32.310 --> 01:30:34.260
look at the faces that are smiling.

01:30:34.260 --> 01:30:36.840
Try right now, spend 10 minutes

01:30:36.840 --> 01:30:39.180
having your eyes focus on those

01:30:39.180 --> 01:30:41.040
and look at those people,"

01:30:41.040 --> 01:30:44.370
that it is an effective intervention

01:30:44.370 --> 01:30:47.520
at improving [sighing] people's
sense of self-efficacy,

01:30:47.520 --> 01:30:48.810
of, "What can I accomplish next?"

01:30:48.810 --> 01:30:50.160
They feel a little bit more energized.

01:30:50.160 --> 01:30:52.830
It doesn't cure depression,
it doesn't cure anxiety.

01:30:52.830 --> 01:30:57.420
I mean, these are literal
physical afflictions that we have,

01:30:57.420 --> 01:30:59.340
so it's not a quick fix,

01:30:59.340 --> 01:31:01.470
but it can produce a temporary change

01:31:01.470 --> 01:31:05.186
that might be a way to start
getting out of that rut.

01:31:05.186 --> 01:31:06.540
- Mm-hmm, yeah, and I think nowadays

01:31:06.540 --> 01:31:09.990
there's an increasing attention on tools

01:31:09.990 --> 01:31:11.190
that will help people orient

01:31:11.190 --> 01:31:14.400
as they start to veer
towards suicidal depression,

01:31:14.400 --> 01:31:17.430
or veer back into a depressive
episode, or anxiety episode.

01:31:17.430 --> 01:31:21.060
I mean, trying to reverse
an entire syndrome

01:31:21.060 --> 01:31:23.220
or set of syndromes is
far more complicated.

01:31:23.220 --> 01:31:24.360
Likewise, in the health space,

01:31:24.360 --> 01:31:26.580
just trying to get people
to deploy realtime tools

01:31:26.580 --> 01:31:31.580
to adjust their anxiety or to
exercise more often and so on.

01:31:31.620 --> 01:31:32.820
As a kind of a final,

01:31:32.820 --> 01:31:34.950
but also kind of a high-level question,

01:31:34.950 --> 01:31:37.047
I'm imagining that,

01:31:37.047 --> 01:31:40.770
and I plan to use this visual
goal setting of spotlighting,

01:31:40.770 --> 01:31:42.840
I've been using it actually
for some time on runs,

01:31:42.840 --> 01:31:43.673
it works really well.

01:31:43.673 --> 01:31:45.540
Yesterday, I took a run
near the waterfront here,

01:31:45.540 --> 01:31:48.720
and I think I did it somewhat incorrectly,

01:31:48.720 --> 01:31:49.800
the entire run, I was thinking about

01:31:49.800 --> 01:31:51.035
getting back to the statue,

01:31:51.035 --> 01:31:52.590
at which I started.
- [laughing] Yeah.

01:31:52.590 --> 01:31:56.100
- But I did find that I ran
fastest in the final 20 meters.

01:31:56.100 --> 01:31:57.090
- Cool.
- Which, admittedly,

01:31:57.090 --> 01:31:58.500
wasn't fast at all,

01:31:58.500 --> 01:32:00.616
but it was faster than what preceded it.

01:32:00.616 --> 01:32:02.190
[Emily laughing]

01:32:02.190 --> 01:32:05.310
So it works and it makes perfect
sense as to how it works.

01:32:05.310 --> 01:32:07.980
You've done other studies

01:32:07.980 --> 01:32:10.200
exploring some of the
other features of vision,

01:32:10.200 --> 01:32:12.570
like the luminosity,
how bright something is

01:32:12.570 --> 01:32:13.500
and how people perceive it.

01:32:13.500 --> 01:32:15.510
That was in a completely
different context,

01:32:15.510 --> 01:32:18.360
but is there a kind of a higher level,

01:32:18.360 --> 01:32:19.650
a kind of a black belt version

01:32:19.650 --> 01:32:20.850
of what we're talking about here,

01:32:20.850 --> 01:32:24.030
where not only am I focusing
on a specific visual location

01:32:24.030 --> 01:32:27.150
as an intermediate or a long-term goal,

01:32:27.150 --> 01:32:29.730
or I'm using an app to ask me a question

01:32:29.730 --> 01:32:30.990
and tap into how I'm feeling,

01:32:30.990 --> 01:32:33.630
create a visual representation
of my motivational state,

01:32:33.630 --> 01:32:37.230
but that I'm also making my
phone as bright as possible,

01:32:37.230 --> 01:32:39.330
I'm also trying to take that visual window

01:32:39.330 --> 01:32:42.150
and actually pay attention
to more of the details

01:32:42.150 --> 01:32:43.530
at that location?

01:32:43.530 --> 01:32:45.513
Or is it simply a matter of kind of,

01:32:46.560 --> 01:32:48.330
in geek speak, visual neuroscience,

01:32:48.330 --> 01:32:50.400
we would just call this
like low spatial frequency,

01:32:50.400 --> 01:32:52.290
just sort of grabbing a
black and white snapshot

01:32:52.290 --> 01:32:54.840
of something here or there in my mind?

01:32:54.840 --> 01:32:58.350
If I attach more detail and
effort to the specific thing

01:32:58.350 --> 01:32:59.580
that I'm focused on,

01:32:59.580 --> 01:33:02.403
is there any evidence that
that's more effective?

01:33:03.330 --> 01:33:05.850
- It certainly changes what our
brains are doing, [laughing]

01:33:05.850 --> 01:33:07.680
so how do we define effectiveness?

01:33:07.680 --> 01:33:10.189
That's a question for
philosophers and that scientists

01:33:10.189 --> 01:33:11.100
will always debate.
- Will it keep me running?

01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:12.560
- Yeah. [laughing]
[Andrew laughing]

01:33:12.560 --> 01:33:14.520
It will when you use it
towards the end of your run,

01:33:14.520 --> 01:33:16.053
just like you've picked up on.

01:33:17.250 --> 01:33:21.540
Yeah, so there's cool
studies that neuroscientists,

01:33:21.540 --> 01:33:23.160
not I, not coming from my lab,

01:33:23.160 --> 01:33:24.150
that neuroscientists have done

01:33:24.150 --> 01:33:26.160
looking at, "What is
it doing to your brain

01:33:26.160 --> 01:33:29.701
when you've decided that you're
going to focus your attention

01:33:29.701 --> 01:33:31.500
on this element of the world

01:33:31.500 --> 01:33:34.110
and not pay attention to something else?

01:33:34.110 --> 01:33:36.420
Is that just sort of
tricking your thoughts

01:33:36.420 --> 01:33:37.680
or is it doing something different,

01:33:37.680 --> 01:33:39.630
to something more basic, more low level?"

01:33:39.630 --> 01:33:41.430
And the answer is yes,

01:33:41.430 --> 01:33:43.320
so there's an area of the brain,

01:33:43.320 --> 01:33:44.910
the fusiform face area,

01:33:44.910 --> 01:33:47.624
it's a part of our brain
that's really specialized

01:33:47.624 --> 01:33:49.110
for making sense of faces.

01:33:49.110 --> 01:33:50.810
It's important as a social species

01:33:51.648 --> 01:33:52.950
to pay attention to other people,

01:33:52.950 --> 01:33:53.940
pay attention to their faces,

01:33:53.940 --> 01:33:55.560
what they're trying to
communicate through their face,

01:33:55.560 --> 01:33:57.120
and so our brain has developed

01:33:57.120 --> 01:34:02.120
a really specialized central
area for doing that then.

01:34:02.820 --> 01:34:04.830
And so these neuroscientists

01:34:04.830 --> 01:34:06.840
will present a face to somebody,

01:34:06.840 --> 01:34:10.800
but superimposed over that is a house

01:34:10.800 --> 01:34:13.590
or something else that is
less special [laughing]

01:34:13.590 --> 01:34:16.620
to us as a social human species.

01:34:16.620 --> 01:34:18.600
And so both of those things,

01:34:18.600 --> 01:34:20.160
because it's sort of like both images

01:34:20.160 --> 01:34:23.673
are sort of transparent,
overlaid over one another,

01:34:26.070 --> 01:34:28.947
our eyes are getting
both of those images in

01:34:28.947 --> 01:34:31.500
and our brain is getting
both of those images in,

01:34:31.500 --> 01:34:35.857
but we can will ourselves
to focus on the house.

01:34:35.857 --> 01:34:37.830
"Just really pay attention
to the features of the house,

01:34:37.830 --> 01:34:40.260
even though everything about
that face is still there too,"

01:34:40.260 --> 01:34:42.120
or, "Pay attention to the face

01:34:42.120 --> 01:34:45.030
and just tell me, what is
it that you are deciding

01:34:45.030 --> 01:34:46.170
that you want to hold on to,

01:34:46.170 --> 01:34:47.820
that you want to look at right now?"

01:34:47.820 --> 01:34:49.860
And you can see that the
brain is responding to that,

01:34:49.860 --> 01:34:51.037
so when people are saying like,

01:34:51.037 --> 01:34:53.580
"I'm really seeing that face,
the details of the face,

01:34:53.580 --> 01:34:55.380
'cause I'm paying attention to the face,"

01:34:55.380 --> 01:34:58.200
even though we know their
eyes are also looking at

01:34:58.200 --> 01:35:00.600
and engaged with the contents of the house

01:35:00.600 --> 01:35:03.000
that's right there, smacked on top,

01:35:03.000 --> 01:35:05.370
the fusiform face area lights up.

01:35:05.370 --> 01:35:06.397
And when they're saying like,

01:35:06.397 --> 01:35:07.890
"Nah, I'm really focused
on the house now,"

01:35:07.890 --> 01:35:10.770
we see activation in the
fusiform face area decline

01:35:10.770 --> 01:35:15.750
and other areas of the brain's
neurological real estate

01:35:15.750 --> 01:35:17.160
start to engage.

